## 1. Introduction
Nuclear fusion is an environmentally benign and sustainable energy source that can satisfy the ever-increasing global demand for energy.[1–5] To alleviate the current energy crisis, various fusion energy projects are operating worldwide.[6] The International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER) has become one of the most famous and promising next-generation energy generators and is being established through massive financial investments from and cooperation among the European Union, the United States, India, Japan, Russia, China, and South Korea.[7] When completed, the ITER will generate considerable energy by burning deuterium $\left(\mathsf{D}_{2}\right)$ and tritium $(\mathrm{T}_{2})$ plasma through the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{{D-T}}}$ fuel cycle $[\mathrm{D}+\mathrm{T}\rightarrow{}^{4}\mathrm{He}+n+17.59~\mathrm{MeV}]$ .[5,8–18] Thus, hydrogen isotopes $\left[\mathrm{H}_{2},\mathrm{D}_{2}\right]$ , and $\mathbf{T}_{2}]$ are important nuclear fuel raw materials, among which $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ is a valuable, recyclable, but scarce fuel that exhibits a high probability of reacting at lower temperatures to generate considerable power in the ITER and other nuclear reactors.[5,19–22] With the imminent advent of nuclear-fusion-based energy production using nuclear fusion reactors, ${\approx}55.6\mathrm{kg}$ of tritium will be required to generate $1000{\mathrm{MW}}$ of energy per year.[23]
Tritium is a radioactive hydrogen isotope that chemically behaves like hydrogen and exhibits a half-life $(t_{1/2})$ of 12.32 years $=3.69\times10^{8}\mathrm{~s}.^{[24]}$ It is an excellent nuclear fuel that has many industrial and scientific applications; however, because $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ is radioactive, it is associated with environmental contamination and health and safety risks.[25] For example, nuclear accidents such as those at the Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant generate thousands of tons of diluted tritiated water (HTO) and its accidental release could harm both human health and the environment.[26–29] Furthermore, large quantities of HTO must be removed and recovered from the environment because of accidental release from nuclear reactors.[29–32] These requirements will stimulate the development of technologies and materials to store $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ safely under ambient conditions and efficiently separate pure tritium from a mixture of hydrogen isotopes with a high separation factor and low energy requirements.[33–35] Owing to the scarcity–high demand tradeoff, $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ must be bred inside a fusion reactor to ensure sustainability and must, therefore, be stored safely and supplied on demand.[20,36] Tritium $(\mathrm{T}_{2})$ is radioactive in nature as well as scarce and extremely expensive. Also, safe facilities and equipment for working with high-specific activity tritium are not available. So, most of the preliminary studies on tritium technologies such as storage and separation applications are usually done using $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as they exhibit nearly identical physiochemical properties of $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ .
Scientists worldwide are developing state-of-the-art metalhydride-based $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ handling equipment, which has been determined as the most suitable for safely storing $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ compactly and at low pressures prior to eventually being separated and recycled, thereby minimizing accidental environmental release, and enhancing reactor operational safety. Various $Z\mathrm{r}.$ -,[37,38] U-,[39] Ti-,[40] and Pd-based[41] and $\mathrm{LaNi}_{5}$ alloys,[42] magnetic (ferromagnetic/paramagnetic) hydrides,[43] and highentropy alloys $(\mathrm{HEAs})^{[5,44-49]}$ have been extensively studied for their tritium storage capabilities; all these alloys exhibit excellent adsorption/desorption at near ambient temperature and low pressures. Additionally, the facile metal hydride technology improves the operational efficiency of many current $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ processing facilities, enabling them to save several million dollars.
Separating hydrogen isotopes, especially $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ in pure form, has recently attracted considerable research attention because it is important for generating fusion energy and is one of the most challenging tasks in modern science and technology. Despite the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ hydrogen isotopes exhibiting large mass differences, separating these isotopes poses a seminal challenge because they exhibit nearly identical sizes, shapes, and thermodynamic properties.[14,16] Extremely high energy consumption, high cost, complex operational procedures, and low selectivity/separation factors are the main limitations of existing isotope separation technologies such as cryogenic distillation,[50] Girdler sulfide processing,[51] and thermal cycling absorption processes (TCAPs).[52] To develop a promising alternative method for separating hydrogen isotopes, several experimental and molecular simulation (MS) studies have revealed that various functional porous materials such zeolites,[15,25,33,53–57] metal organic frameworks (MOFs),[8,9,13,14,58–64] covalent organic frameworks (COFs),[65] nanoporous carbons,[66,67] metal organic cages,[68] and porous organic cages[69] exhibiting high porosity, ultrasmall pores, and diverse functionalities can selectively capture and separate hydrogen isotopes by relying on “quantum sieving” (QS). In porous materials such as zeolites and MOFs, hydrogen isotopes separation can be realized with high selectivity through “kinetic quantum sieving” (KQS) and “chemical affinity quantum sieving” (CAQS).[8,9,13–15,25,33,53–64]
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) (e.g., MFU-4l;[59] MIL53(Al);[61] and Fe-, Co-, and Ni-MOF-74[58]), constructed using ligands and metal nodes or clusters, have been extensively studied by various research groups for successful and efficient separation of hydrogen isotopologues owing to their unique properties such as high specific surface areas, well-defined tunable pore structures, structural diversity, high concentration of open metal site (OMS), and the potential to introduce strong adsorption sites to frameworks. $\mathrm{KQS^{[9,61]}}$ and $\mathsf{C A Q S}^{[13,58,60]}$ are the well-proposed separation mechanisms in MOF, based on which $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivities in excess of 10 have been reported. Winrauch et al. developed the $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -MFU-4l MOF as a promising material for selectively capturing $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ from low-concentration hydrogen gas mixtures; the MOF exhibited a maximum $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 11.1 at $100~\mathrm{K}$ .[60] More importantly, Kim et al. strategically combined KQS and CAQS in MOF-74-IM- $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and reported highest $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ separation factor of ${\approx}26$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and experimentally validated the practical application of this MOF to industrially isolate highpurity $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ through direct selective separation studies using 1:1 $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixtures and this strategy is applicable to separate $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ from a hydrogen isotopic mixture.[13] Recently, flexible MOFs exhibiting reversible structural transformations have been developed as a new subclass of MOFs, which exhibit excellent selectivity towards hydrogen isotope separation.[61,70–72]
Zeolites, highly crystalline inorganic microporous aluminosilicate nanomaterials, have attracted considerable research attention for systematically separating and adsorbing hydrogen isotopologues at a technological scale due to their unprecedented structural features such as network of linked cavities and complex intrapores (e.g., interconnected cylindrical pore structures in the range $3{-}9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}]$ ), and exposed framework cations in addition to good thermal and radiation stability.[15,16,20,25,53,56,73–76] Various experimental and simulation studies have been carried out on LTA,[53] RHO,[55] Faujasite,[77] MFI,[25,56] CHA,[78] and $\mathsf{B C T}^{[20]}$ type zeolites reported the efficient separation, storage, and recovery of $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{2}$ , HD, HT, $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , DT, and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ . Most of the reports have indicated that $\mathrm{KQS}^{[20]}$ and CAQS[25,56,57,78] play a vital role in efficient hydrogen isotope separations. Recently, Hirscher et al. explored the excellent potential of metal-ion-exchanged zeolites towards hydrogen isotope separation in which the selectivity was greatly influenced by the density of ion-exchanged metal sites.[25,56,57] Xiong et al. achieved the highest $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 24.9, ever measured, using unsaturated $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -exchanged sites in ZSM-5 zeolite at $100\mathrm{K}$ for $\mathrm{1{:}1\ D_{2}/H_{2}}$ gas based on CAQS.[56] Whereas, a $\mathrm{T}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ selectivity of 3.7 at 77 K was also predicted for $\mathrm{Ag(I)}$ -ZSM-5, suggesting that metal-ion-exchanged zeolites may be used to enrich and separate tritium from hydrogen isotopic mixtures in a technologically favorable temperature range.[25]
Other 2-D layered materials such as graphene, hexagonal boron nitride (h-BN), and MXenes have unique physicochemical features including atomically thin layers, numerous active sites and functional groups on basal planes and edges, very large surface areas, and high surface-area-to-volume ratios.[79–82] Additionally, 2-D layered materials have attracted considerable research interest in the hydrogen isotope storage and separation field because these materials intrinsically exhibit numerous uniformly sized pores and angstrom-range interlayer galleries, which act as selective permeation/sieving passages and confined spaces,[80,83] respectively, for quantum confinement in one principal direction and molecular transport and storage. Graphene and h-BN monolayers exhibit subatomic selectivity and high room-temperature thermal proton permeability and can be used to separate hydrogen isotopes.[79,80,84,85] Geim et al. reported that graphene and h-BN separate hydrogen isotopes through quantum confinement with an H-over-D selectivity of 10 at room temperature and suggested that a tritium–hydrogen separation factor of 30 could be achieved using an atomically thin graphene membrane by implementing electrochemical pumping.[28] Recently, An et al. estimated that $\mathrm{H^{+}/T^{+}}$ selectivity of 12 could be achieved using graphene sheets exhibiting Stone-Wales (SW) defects under ambient conditions.[35] In addition to graphene and h-BN, 2-D layer-structured graphene-like MXene materials exhibiting unique physiochemical properties have attracted considerable research interest as candidates for application to reversible hydrogen storage under ambient conditions and for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas separation. These properties include their exceptional surface-tunable chemical compatibility and compositional flexibility, regular interlamellar channels, abundant functional groups, and radiation resistance.[86,87] Recently, Liu et al. used titanium carbide $(\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{x})$ MXene stacks to achieve an unprecedented hydrogen uptake of $8.8\mathrm{wt\%}$ at room temperature and an $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pressure of 60 bar.[88] Using $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ membranes, Fan et al.[82] reported an $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 41 at $320^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and Ding et al.[89] reported an $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ selectivity of ${>}160$ . These performances surpassed those of previous state-of-the-art membranes and may be attributed to regular nanometer channels. These findings demonstrate that 2-D layered graphene, h-BN, and MXenes are excellent candidates for application to hydrogen isotope storage and separation because of their unique chemistries, layered structures, and interstitial spaces.
This review summarizes the latest groundbreaking studies, recent progress, and key strategies that could improve hydrogen isotope storage and separation while emphasizing tritium isotopes and focusing on metal hydrides, nanoporous zeolites, and MOFs and on 2-D layered graphene, h-BN, and MXenes (Figure 1). First, we describe the recent progress in tritium storage using representative metal hydrides such as intermetallic alloys, HEAs, and magnetic (ferromagnetic/paramagnetic) hydride nanomaterials (which have been utilized in nuclear fusion reactors), and the challenges of storing tritium under ambient conditions. Then, we provide an overview of the important developments and statuses of functional porous zeolites and MOFs toward hydrogen isotope separation based on zeolite and MOF morphologies and experimentally measured and computationally predicted KQS and CAQS mechanisms at confined nanopores. Subsequently, we highlight the impacts of nanopores/defects and well-defined nanochannels in 2-D layered materials such as graphene, h-BN, and MXenes toward efficient $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ separation based on recent reports. Finally, future hydrogen isotope storage and separation perspectives are reviewed.
## 2. Importance of Fusion Energy, Tritium Inventories, and Deuterium–Tritium (D–T) Fuel Cycle
Achieving global net zero emissions of carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ by 2050 requires curbing the growth in energy demand alongside a radical change in the energy mix, involving a wholesale shift to nuclear, renewable, and other clean energy sources and technologies. By 2050, nuclear energy will account for $12\%$ of the global total primary energy supply (Figure 2a).[90] Currently, there are 442 nuclear power reactors in operation in 32 countries generating $393{\mathrm{~GW}}({\mathrm{e}})$ of electricity and providing continuous and reliable low carbon power.[18] More importantly, nuclear power is reducing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions by about two gigatons per year.[18] Therefore, building a fusion reactor, achieving a self-sustaining fusion reaction and converting that power to near-inexhaustible electricity will be a game-changer in the quest to achieve a low carbon future and combat global warming.
Renewable energy (solar, wind, hydroelectric, geothermal, and tidal waves, etc.), in addition to nuclear energy, is also recognized as a contributor to reducing carbon emissions.[91] Nevertheless, both nuclear and renewable energies have aroused considerable debate about their respective pros and cons. The power attributes of nuclear and renewable energy are very different. Renewable sources, such as solar and wind energy, and zero carbon-emitting renewable-based technologies with a low lifecycle footprint have been proven to be intermittent, unsustainable, and low-power, owing to their reliance on natural forces.[92] In contrast, nuclear boasts the ability to provide a continuous source of energy. When there is a gap in renewable energy production, small nuclear plants could plug the gap in a way that is not possible for larger nuclear projects because of their high cost-to-energy ratio. Small modular reactors (SMRs), which generate less than $300\mathrm{MW},$ or even as little as $10\mathrm{MW},$ , are the most promising next-generation nuclear technologies which could extend the market for nuclear energy by providing power to smaller grid systems or isolated markets where larger nuclear plants are not suitable.[93] New advances in smaller, more affordable reactors may also expand the potential markets for nuclear hybrid energy systems (HESs) by creating more flexible and affordable options for integrated system deployment. Moreover, in terms of cleaner production, the carbon-free SMR and renewable technologies can be integrated and form a nuclear-renewable hybrid energy system (N-R-HES) to leverage the benefits of each technology, providing reliable, sustainable electricity to the grid low-carbon energy to other energy use sectors.[94] Nuclear-renewable HESs can offer various applications, such as desalination, hydrogen production, district heating or cooling, the extraction of tertiary oil resources and process heat applications, such as cogeneration, coal-to-liquid conversion and assistance in the synthesis of chemical feedstock.[95]
Fusion research has a well-defined roadmap, which still requires major international R&D efforts to realize fusion energy around the middle of this century. Tokamaks have already produced fusion power close to or just above the break-even condition, successfully generating $16~\mathrm{MW}$ of fusion power, for 24 MW of power used to heat the plasma (a Q ratio of 0.67). International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER), the key facility of the roadmap (Figure 2b), is the world’s largest and most advanced experimental nuclear fusion reactor facility.[23] ITER is designed to use deuterium–tritium nuclear fusion reaction to produce a net surplus of fusion energy (fusion gain $Q=10$ ) of about $500~\mathrm{MW}$ for an injected power of $50~\mathrm{MW}$ whose full power operation will now begin in 2035.[18,96] According to the roadmap leading to the realization of fusion electricity, ITER should be followed by a prototype for a power-producing fusion reactor called DEMO, a $2{\-}4\ \operatorname{G}\mathbb{W}$ demonstration fusion power plant. DEMO fusion would supply approximately $1\%$ of the world energy demand by around 2070.[97] More importantly, the ITER and nextgeneration DEMO fusion reactors require large amounts of $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ for test/initial startup and will burn kilograms of $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ annually for their operation.[98] The total $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ inventory required is one of the most important key issues for fusion energy development in the future. The realization of fusion energy is faced with a number of challenges, among which self-sufficiency in scarce $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ is prominent.
## 2.1. Tritium Inventories
Tritium, with a half-life of 12.3 years, is the prime fuel used in fusion reactors, and is hardly found in nature.[99,100] Fusion reactors generally need a large startup $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ supply. If this quantity is very large, the availability of $\mathrm{\ddot{T}}_{2}$ from other global sources may not be sufficient to meet the demand.[101] Within the tritium plant of ITER, a total inventory of about $2{-}3~\mathrm{kg}$ will be necessary to operate the machine in the D–T phase with a tritium throughput of up to $0.5~\mathrm{kg~h}^{-1}$ depending on the chosen fueling scenario and burn pulse operation.[102] The available tritium stockpile— thought to be now about $25~\mathrm{Kg}$ today—will peak before the end the decade and begin a steady decline (Figure 2c) as it is sold off and decays, according to projections in ITER’s 2018 research plan.[100] The world’s only commercial sources are Canada Deuterium Uranium (CANDU) nuclear reactors whose operation in the next decades (both in terms of extending the life of existing reactors and constructing new ones) is now a matter of debate.[99] Owing to the large uncertainty and complex issues surrounding the variety of potential $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ supply sources, there is a possibility that external $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ will be unavailable for the start-up of the DEMO fusion reactor around 2050.[96] In a worst-case scenario, “it would appear that there is insufficient tritium to satisfy the fusion demand after ITER.”[100] As the tritium inventory for the fuel-cycle start-up is a vital parameter, its sufficiency is one of the key issues remaining to be solved in upcoming fusion reactors.[23]
## 2.2. D–T Fuel Cycle of a Fusion Reactor
The development of fusion energy systems is focused on the D–T fusion cycle.[103] The main fuels for controlled fusion (Equation 1) are the hydrogen isotopes, i.e., deuterium (D) and tritium (T).[102]
$$
\mathrm{D}+\mathrm{T}\rightarrow\alpha+n\left(\mathrm{~}Q_{\mathrm{DT}}=17.59\mathrm{~MeV}\right)
$$
The fuel cycle of a fusion reactor is normally subdivided into the inner fuel cycle (IFC) and the outer fuel cycle (OFC)
(Figure 2d).[103] The IFC is necessary to recycle and purify the un-burnt tritium fraction in order to achieve a net energy gain (i.e., power injected to run the plasma and the fusion power liberated by the D–T fusion). The IFC systems, designed to process considerable and unprecedented D–T flow rates with high flexibility and reliability, include a plasma exhaust (vacuum pump), an exhaust processing system (EPS), an isotope separation system (ISS), a water detritiation system, a storage delivery system (SDS), and fueling systems.[104,105] The OFC, by contrast, serves to efficiently extract the bred tritium, which includes breeding blankets, and the coolant purification and tritium extraction and recovery systems.[106] Thus, the EPS, ISS, and SDS are key systems for both loops.
In a D–T fusion reactor, deuterium and tritium are continuously fueled into a torus or tokamak system. The tokamak exhaust is a complex, multi-species mixture consisting of all six hydrogen isotopologues $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ , HD, HT, $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , DT, and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ ), plasma enhancement gases comprising inert noble gases, and $^3\mathrm{He}$ from the decay of tritium. Since only deuterium and tritium must be recycled, the exhaust gas must be recovered, refined, isotopically separated, and refueled to maintain the fuel efficiency.[106] The off-gases from the torus collected in cryopumps are moved by the roughing pump into the EPS. The EPS cleans up the exhaust gas and removes plasma enhancement gases and other impurities from the hydrogen isotopes. In the EPS, hydrogen isotopologues are extracted from the other gases and immediately diverted to the ISS which can handle only highly purified hydrogen isotopes, while the remaining detritiated waste gas is sent to a detritiation system for decontamination purposes before release into the environment. The ISS receives a hydrogen stream from the blankets that contain tritium but are not yet in fuel quality.[106] The main task of the ISS is the production of a protium-free tritium stream for fueling. The ISS is linked directly to the SDS. The SDS stores deuterium and tritium in isotopic purities as produced by the ISS in metal hydride beds. The SDS is connected to the fueling system where deuterium and tritium are prepared to fuel plasma. The operations of all the subsystems of the inner tritium fuel cycle are strongly inter-linked. Normally, the addition of an OFC is based on the declared goal of the fusion reactor, where the tritium-breeding mechanism is essential to demonstrate tritium self-sufficiency. In this case, several solid breeder blanket modules, which have lithium compounds as a tritium-breeding material and beryllium compounds as a neutron multiplier, are installed surrounding a reactor core.[107] Tritium is bred in the blanket modules due to the nuclear reactions of neutrons and lithium, which must be extracted. The extracted tritium should also be processed through OFC before fueling into the reactor. A separate ISS has been designed to ease the burden of the inner cycling loop, because the features of hydrogen isotope separation between the IFC and the OFC are very different, in that an approximately equal percentage of deuterium and tritium is found in the exhaust of the IFC, whereas there is almost no deuterium in the OFC.[23]
## 2.2.1. Isotope Separation System (ISS)
Deuterium and tritium are key energy sources for fusion reactions. The separation of the hydrogen isotopes, deuterium $\left(\mathrm{D}=\mathbf{\Omega}^{2}\mathrm{H}\right)$ and tritium $(\mathrm{T}=\mathrm{\Omega}^{3}\mathrm{H}).$ ) from nuclear waste in a highly pure form is of prime importance in the operation of nuclear fusion reactors. This is a challenge and requires special modern separation technologies because of their nearly identical size, shape, and thermodynamic properties rendering them difficult to separate. In the D–T fuel cycle of the fusion reactors, the consumption ratio of the fuels $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ in the torus is less than $10\%$ , and the unreacted exhaust gas discharged from plasma after a partial fusion reaction consists of all possible combination of hydrogen isotopes that should be treated in order to recycle $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ used as fuel.[108] The separation of isotope mixtures is possible only with a limited number of techniques such as cryogenic distillation or electrolysis of heavy water produced by the Girdler sulfide process.[9,12,16,109] The Girdler sulfide process separates water isotopologues through the chemical reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $+\mathrm{HDS}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{HDO}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{\mathrm S}$ in a closed loop and provides $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ by additional $\mathtt{D}_{2}\mathtt{O}$ electrolysis.[109] Meanwhile, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ distillation is based on the difference in the boiling temperatures of isotopes $(20.3\mathrm{~K~}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $23.7\mathrm{K}$ for $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ ).[16,109] However, producing valuable tritium and deuterium through these techniques creates immense economic value, as well as being energy-intensive processes, that require multiple steps, and yield a low separation factor (Girdler sulfide process: ${\approx}2$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ distillation: 1.5 at 24 K).[109] It is therefore imperative to explore large and continuous hydrogen ISS for the construction of fusion reactors (ITER and future DEMO facilities). Currently, hydrogen isotope separation based on porous and layered materials has been considered as a promising alternative to the existing conventional separation methods. Porous materials such as zeolites and MOFs are considered to be promising isotope-sieving materials due to their well-defined, tunable pore structure and the potential to introduce strong adsorption sites directly into the framework structure in which the hydrogen isotope separation takes place based on quantum effects (KQS or CAQS). In addition, 2D layered materials (graphene, h-BN, and MXenes) that exhibit unique nanopores and nanochannels are considered as most promising to separate hydrogen isotopes by sieving through either the nanopores or the interstitial space between the atomic planes.
## 2.2.2. Storage and Delivery Systems (SDS)
With the development of the ITER project, it is reported that the tritium requirement will be $1,000{-}1,200\mathrm{g}$ per year between 2025 and 2038.[5,98] On the basis of the minimum tritium storage design principle, a total of $2{-}3\mathrm{Kg}$ of tritium pre-stored is required to run the machine in the D–T phase for each plant of the ITER.[102] Thus, the development of tritium storage materials for SDSs that are safe, reliable, and of high efficiency plays a vital role in the normal operation of the ITER. Certain metals and alloys, which react rapidly and reversibly with hydrogen at room temperature and atmospheric pressure to form metal hydride compounds, represent a most common, safe, and well-established technology ideally suited to the handling and processing of tritium. One of the most important advantages of using hydride technology in tritium processes is the compact size of hydride equipment. Till date, various metals (U, Pd, Ti, and Zr), intermetallic alloys $(\mathrm{ZrCo},\mathrm{LiAl}_{5})$ ), magnetic alloys and HEAs have been considered as candidate tritium storage materials. Nevertheless, the use of these materials for tritium storage has major drawbacks such as i) inhibition of the hydriding reaction owing to the release of $^3\mathrm{He}$ from tritium decay; ii) the disproportionation/pyrophoric nature of the materials; and iii) the requirement of high a temperature to desorb the tritium from the metal which leads to tritium permeation concerns. To overcome these limitations, extensive R&D would be required.
## 3. Tritium $(\overline{{\mathsf{T}}}_{2})$ Storage and Separation Using Metal Hydrides
Metal hydrides are suitable materials for storing and delivering hydrogen isotopes such as tritium.[38,110] They exhibit an extremely high hydrogen volumetric absorption density, store hydrogen several times more compactly than other high-pressure gases and are often more compact than liquid hydrogen. Thus, in fusion reactors and tritium facilities, tritium is stored as metal tritides (e.g., tritium-containing hydrides) (Figure 3) according to the following reactions (Equation 2):
$$
\mathrm{~\cal~M~}(\mathrm{s})+\frac{x}{2}\mathrm{~{\cal~Q}~}_{2}\mathrm{~\tiny~(~g)~}\frac{\mathrm{absorption}}{\mathrm{desorption}}{\mathrm{M}}\mathrm{Q}_{x}\mathrm{~(s)~(Q~=~H,~D,~or~T)~}
$$
The stored tritium is eventually separated and recycled. Compactly storing tritium at low pressures curtails accidental tritium release, which ensures the operational safety of tritium handling facilities while reducing the equipment size. More importantly, most hydride systems can be automated and remotely operated and controlled, which is a very important advantage for both facilities handling nuclear materials and for future nuclear fusion and hydrogen energy systems. The facile metal hydride technology improves the efficiency of many current tritium operations and annually saves several million dollars in operating cost in the nuclear energy industry. Thus, researchers and fusion scientists worldwide are developing state-of-the-art metal-hydridebased tritium-handling equipment.
## 3.1. ZrCo Tritides
Of all the hydride-forming metals and metal alloys, $\mathrm{\Delta{Zr}\mathrm{Co}}$ is one of the most promising materials to handle, transport, and store tritium in the fuel cycle of fusion devices such as the ITER SDS and hydride beds. This is because $\mathrm{ZrCo}$ exhibits low pyrophoricity, low room-temperature equilibrium adsorption pressure, negligible volumetric expansion upon hydriding, and relatively a higher hydrogen storage capacity.[22,37,111,112] Additionally, $\boldsymbol{Z}\mathrm{r}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Co}}$ is nonnuclear, releases tritium at relatively low temperatures, and can trap $^3\mathrm{He}$ generated by tritium decay. However, $\mathrm{ZrCoH}_{3}$ is disproportionate into highly stable $\mathrm{ZrH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{ZrCo}_{2}$ phases, which greatly attenuates the $\mathrm{zrCo}$ hydrogen storage capacity and cycling stability after repeated re/dehydrogenations (Figure 4a).[111] Hence, it constitutes an important problem for safely handling tritium at tritium processing plants and thermonuclear fusion reactors and gradually reduces the SDS system efficiency.[37,111] Therefore, the disproportionation of $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{zr}C o}$ must be inhibited for application to hydrogen isotope $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2},\operatorname{D}_{2}\right)$ , and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ ) storage according to the following reactions (Equations 3 and 4):
$$
2\mathrm{ZrCoH}_{3}\rightarrow\mathrm{ZrCo}_{2}+\mathrm{ZrH}_{2}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}
$$
$$
2\mathrm{Zr}{\mathrm{Co}}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{Zr}{\mathrm{Co}}_{2}+\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{H}_{2}
$$
To restrain or eliminate disproportionation, substituting elements such as Ti,[112–115] Mn, $\mathtt{C u}$ ,[116] Fe,[117] $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ ,[118,119] Nb,[120] Sc, V, Mo, or $\mathrm{Hf}^{\{10,121,122\}}$ in $\mathsf{Z r C o}$ alloys decreases the $\boldsymbol{Z}\mathrm{r}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Co}}$ lattice parameter and cell volume, which impedes ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r}{\cal C}\mathrm{o-H}$ disproportionation, enhances hydrogen storage, and prolongs the material lifetime (Table S1, Supporting Information).[22,111,123] Among the examined substitution elements, $\mathrm{Hf.}$ and Ti-modified $\mathrm{ZrCo}$ alloys such as $\mathrm{Zr}_{1-x}\mathrm{Hf}_{x}\mathrm{Co}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}_{1-x}\mathrm{Ti}_{x}\mathrm{Co}$ are reportedly advantageous for handling tritium in the ITER. [113,124,125] To develop a superior ITER h e storage bed, Kou et al. fabricated full-scale thin double-layered $\mathrm{ZrCo}.$ -, $\mathrm{Zr_{0.8}H f_{0.2}C o-}$ , and $\mathrm{Zr_{0.8}T i_{0.2}C o}$ -loaded They reported that the $\mathrm{Zr}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ti}_{0.2}\mathrm{Co}$ bed demonstrated superior hydrogen delivery properties luding higher $\lvert\approx18\mathrm{~mol~}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ up to $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ (Figure 4b) and faster hydrogen delivery and better cyclic delivery while maintainin good structural stability, indicating that the proposed $\mathrm{Zr_{0.8}T i_{0.2}C o}$ bed was a good candidate for application to ITER hydrogen isotop recovery and delivery.[10] To improve the storage and safe handling of the tritium and deuterium inventories in the ITER SDS fuel cycle, Yao et al. developed $\mathrm{zr}$ -MNV-based $\mathsf{Z r M n}_{2-x}\mathsf{V}_{x}$ $(x=0$ , 0.4, 0.6, or 0.8) and $\mathrm{ZrMn}_{1.4-\gamma}\mathrm{V}_{0.6}$ $(\gamma=0$ , 0.2, or 0.4) alloys by partially substituting ZrCo Co atoms with Mn atoms and larger $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ atoms.[126] They reported that $\mathrm{ZrMn}_{1.2}\mathrm{V}_{0.6}$ exhibited an outstanding stable hydrogen storage capacity of $1.63~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Figure 4d) compared with pure $\mathrm{ZrCo}$ during 20 cycles and that the storage capacity scarcely decreased during anti-disproportionation measurements in the simulated SDS environment. Because $\mathrm{ZrMn}_{1.2}\mathrm{V}_{0.6}$ exhibited anti-disproportionation performance compared to $\mathrm{ZrCo}$ , which is crucial for ITER application, $\mathrm{ZrMn}_{1.2}\mathrm{V}_{0.6}$ is a promising alternative material for application to SDS hydrogen isotope storage and delivery. In addition, these results suggest that decreasing the lattice volumetric expansion during hydrogenation is beneficial for stabilizing to the $\mathrm{ZrCo}$ structure, which detains long-range atomic migration and is favorable for improving antidisproportionation and, thus, achieving a highly stable storage capacity in ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r}{\cal C}\mathrm{o-H}$ systems. To restrain disproportionation, Liang et al. tuned the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZrCo}}$ lattice by substituting Pd for Co and investigated $\mathrm{ZrCo}_{0.6}\mathrm{Pd}_{0.4}$ dehydrogenation/hydrogenation which exhibited a maximum storage capacity of $1.45~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Figure 4e).[37] Also, the $\boldsymbol{Z}\mathrm{r}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Co}}$ and $\mathrm{ZrCo}_{0.6}\mathrm{Pd}_{0.4}$ hydrogenation expansivities were $23.5\%$ and $15.7\%$ after 50 cycles, respectively, indicating that the $\mathrm{ZrCo}_{0.6}\mathrm{Pd}_{0.4}$ lattice structure had stabilized and that the disproportionation-free volumetric expansion had decreased (Figure 4f). Moreover, The $\mathrm{ZrCo}$ and $\mathrm{ZrCo}_{0.6}\mathrm{Pd}_{0.4}$ cycling stabilities are investigated for 50 hydriding–dehydriding cycles, the dehydrogenation capacity only decreased from 1.5 to $1.45~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . Moreover, the capacity retention was up to $96.67\%$ , which was higher than any previously reported modified $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZrCo}}$ -based alloy, suggesting the great potential of lattice tuning to improve the cycling performance of hydrogen isotope storage materials. $\mathrm{ZrCo}$ films modified with a thin Ti layer are usually used as tritium targets.[113] For instance, Luo et al. designed double-layer $\mathsf{Z r C o/T i}$ composite films and achieved an unexpectedly high storage capacity $(1.85~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and rapid hydrogen absorption and high thermal stability, indicating the potential of developing and applying tritiated film targets to neutron generator apparatus.[115] To ensure safe tritium handling, recovery, transport, and delivery, fast absorption/desorption kinetics are required to confirm low permanent tritium trapping. However, $\boldsymbol{Z}\mathrm{r}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Co}}$ is limited by its high activation energy requirement. Adding Mn should enhance the activation barrier;[127] therefore, Weng et al. prepared $\mathrm{ZrCo}_{1-x}\mathrm{Mn}_{x}$ $\boldsymbol{x}=0$ , 0.025, 0.05, 0.075, or 0.1) alloys by partially substituting Mn for Co and reported that the initial $\mathrm{ZrCo}_{1-x}\mathrm{Mn}_{x}$ activation behavior improved with increasing Mn content. All the alloys absorbed hydrogen ( $1.956\%$ , $1.958\%$ , $1.959\%$ , $1.925\%$ , and $1.697\%$ respectively) and saturated within $200~\mathrm{s}$ , which implies that the main $\boldsymbol{Z}\mathrm{r}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Co}}$ activation drawback had been overcome by adding Mn.[128]
## 3.2. Uranium (U), Titanium (Ti), and Palladium (Pd) Tritides
Uranium is a technically feasible and the most widely used metal tritide for handling tritium because uranium exhibits favorable characteristics such as hydrogen intake and release at $<10^{-3}$ Pa and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and because it holds tritium tightly over a wide pressure-composition (P-c) isotherm. However, uranium is radioactive, and hydrogenated uranium fragments into a spontaneously combusting fine powder, which impedes the practical application of uranium.[22] To resolve this issue, uranium alloys such as U–Al, U–Mo, U–Cr, $\mathrm{U}{-}\mathrm{Zr}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ $\mathrm{U}(\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Al}_{1-x})$ , UFeAl, and UCoAl have been developed for application to tritium storage. Takuya et al. used $\mathrm{U}_{2}\mathrm{Ti}$ over the range $298\substack{-973\mathrm{~K~}}$ and at hydrogen pressures below $10^{5}$ Pa and achieved a hydrogen storage capacity of 7.6 atoms per formula unit.[129] Moreover, $\mathrm{U}_{2}\mathrm{Ti}$ exhibited high durability against powdering, making it highly suitable for application to tritium storage, Yamawaki et al. used firstprinciples calculations to analyze the crystal structures and lattice constants of various U-based alloys.[39] They predicted that UNiAl could be a promising hydrogen isotope storage system owing to its low absorption and desorption temperatures and superior powdering resistance. Titanium is inexpensive and is recommended for long-term tritium storage because it adsorbs two tritium atoms per Ti atom at a very low tritium pressure of approximately $1\mathrm{E}^{-7}$ torr. Moreover, it is stable in air up to approximately $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and not pyrophoric in air at room temperature. Nevertheless, titanium requires a high temperature to desorb tritium. To find the most suitable titanium alloy composition for tritium recovery and storage, Nomura and Akiba[40] tested $26\ \mathrm{Ti}$ $(33-47\mathrm{mol}\%)\mathrm{V}(42-67\mathrm{mol}\%)\mathrm{Fe}(0-14\mathrm{mol}\%)$ alloys and reported that $\mathrm{Ti}_{43.5}\mathrm{V}_{49.0}\mathrm{Fe}_{7.5}$ exhibited a maximum hydrogen adsorption of $3.90~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $253\ \mathrm{K}$ Among the Ti–V–X $[\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Co, Ni, Cr, or Pd) alloy series, iron was the best substituent element. Palladium (Pd) and Pd-alloy membranes are widely employed in the ITER, into which deuterium and tritium mixtures are fed in a particular $\mathrm{{D/T}}$ ratio for storing, extracting, purifying, and separating hydrogen isotopes, because Pd and its alloys exhibit oxidation and poisoning resistance and rapid hydrogen isotope absorption and desorption kinetics. More importantly, Pd alloys can accumulate and retain helium generated by tritium decay (during aging) for more than 16 years, which influences hydrogen isotope separation factors and must, therefore, be thoroughly studied by applying Pd alloys to tritium storage. Thiébaut et al.[130] and Zhu et al.[131] experimentally investigated the effect of aging up to 9 and 3 years on Pd $\mathsf{P-c}$ isotherms for tritium storage in $\mathrm{Pd}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{T}_{2}$ , at different temperatures and reported that the hydrogen storage properties were remarkably well-preserved during aging and that the $\mathsf{P-c}$ isotherms had only slightly changed. In contrast, Liu et al. experimentally measured separation factors $(\alpha)$ for the palladium–hydrogen system aged using tritium in the range $248\substack{-303\mathrm{~K~}}$ up to 3.5 years and reported that $\alpha$ decreased in the aged Pd–H–D system compared to $\alpha$ in the fresh one, which may be because helium had weakened the hydrogen isotope effect.[132] In addition, $\alpha$ decreased more with increasing aging temperature. Recently, Du et al. reported that nanoporous palladium exhibiting ${\approx}5~\mathrm{nm}$ pores exhibited higher deuterium/hydrogen isotope separation factors up to $323\mathrm{~K~}$ and, therefore, should be a superior hydrogen isotope separation material.[41]
## 3.3. LaNi5 Alloys
Intermetallic $\mathrm{LaNi}_{5}$ is a promising tritium storage material because it can form reversible hydrides with hydrogen isotopes and because of its rapid kinetics and strong helium retainability.[133,134] As favored materials, the ${\mathrm{LaNi}}_{5-x}{\mathrm{Al}}_{x}$ alloy series (especially $\mathrm{LaNi}_{4.25}\mathrm{Al}_{0.75})$ ) have been used as tritium storage media for more than 20 years, especially in tritium-processing facilities that store tritium at the Savannah River Site (SRS) in the USA.[135,136] Furthermore, Wang et al. reported that $\mathrm{LaNi}_{4.25}\mathrm{Al}_{0.75}$ exhibited rapid tritium adsorption and desorption and easy activation and did not require a long time to equilibrate, indicating that the material was suitable for storing hydrogen isotopes, especially tritium, and for pumping tritium during the entire $^3\mathrm{He}$ capture process.[133] In addition, because Mg-based alloys are economical to manufacture and exhibit excellent heat resistance, recyclability, and reversibility, Lü et al. anticipated improved tritium storage properties by partially substituting $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ for La in $\mathrm{LaNi}_{4.25}\mathrm{Al}_{0.75}$ alloys and studied the tritium storage potential of as-cast $\mathrm{La}_{(1-x)}\mathrm{Mg}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{4.25}\mathrm{Al}_{0.75}$ ( $\left[{{x}=0.0}\right.$ , 0.1, 0.2, or 0.3) alloys.[137] The authors reported that the alloy absorption kinetics increased with increasing $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ content from 0.1 to 0.3, which implied that developing tritium storage alloys exhibiting good kinetics is beneficial. By anticipating the increase in lattice volume when Al was substituted for Ni, Liu et al. prepared a series of ${\mathrm{LaNi}}_{5-x}{\mathrm{Al}}_{x}$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=0$ , 0.25, 0.5, or 0.75) alloys and predicted that $\mathrm{LaNi}_{5}$ hydrogen (tritium/deuterium) absorption/desorption could be improved by adjusting the covalent interaction between H and Ni through alloying.[42]
## 3.4. Magnetic (Ferromagnetic/Paramagnetic) Hydrides
In addition to storing hydrogen isotopes, metal hydrides have potential to efficiently separate hydrogen isotopes. Ito et al. conducted hydrogen isotope separation experiments with the ferromagnetic $\mathtt{L a C o}_{5}$ material under the influence of magnetic field and reported that the separation factor $(\alpha=\mathrm{(D/H)_{gas}/(D/H)_{s o l i d}})$ was increased from $1.54$ at zero field to 2.25 at $13\mathrm{T}$ for the system $\mathrm{LaCo}_{5^{-}}(\mathrm{H}_{2}+\mathrm{D}_{2})$ .[138] Recently, Murph et al. demonstrated that using magnetic-hydride materials to incorporate alternating electromagnetic fields (AEMFs) was an efficient method for targeting and methodically releasing hydrogen isotopes from metal hydrides.[43] AEMF should lead to sufficiently rapid, reversible, and energy-efficient hydrogen absorption and desorption, which are highly desirable for practical applications. Murph et al. used magnetic metal hydride nanomaterials to store hydrogen and release protium and deuterium.[43] The authors designed iron oxide–palladium $(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{Pd})$ hybrid magnetic hydride nanomaterials (Figure 5a) and assessed their hydrogen isotope sorption/desorption in an AEMF. By tuning the magnetic field strength, the authors reported that protium and deuterium exhibited similar loading capacities $(\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{M})$ of 0.55 and 0.45 (Figure 5b), respectively, and considerably different release kinetics during magnetic heating. A series of highly reproducible magnetically induced charge–discharge cycles indicated that Pd retained the isotopic effect, which proved the robustness of the technique. Furthermore, Murph et al. utilized a $425\mathrm{kHz}$ AEMF to release hydrogen isotopes and to control and selectively release hydrogen isotopes (protium and deuterium) from $\mathrm{LaNi}_{4.25}\mathrm{Al}_{0.75}$ lanthanum–nickel–aluminum (LANA) materials (Figure 5c).[139] The authors reported that $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ had desorbed 1.5 times faster than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and that the LANA adsorption capacity was retained without decreasing, even after 100 absorption–desorption cycles (Figure 5d).[139] Furthermore, the importance of this study was the strict interaction between the AEMF and LANA material, which facilitated selective zone heating and is pertinent to hydrogen isotope separation and refinement. Thus, the versatility of the controlled, targeted, and noncontact AEMF for processing tritium or storing hydrogen in metal hydride units should reduce the complexity of heat exchanger requirements and considerably reduce fusion reactor operating costs. Another benefit of this technology is that materials can be rapidly heated while the surrounding area is not subjected to extreme heat loading.
## 3.5. High-Entropy Alloys (HEAs)
Considerable tritium $(8~\times~10^{5}$ atoms/fission) can be released from nuclear-powered devices and fission reactors into the environment through hydrogen embrittlement posing severe safety hazards, hence, tritium permeation and retention should be emphasized.[5,45–48] Recently, HEAs (e.g., TiVZrNbHf, ZrTiVCrFeNi, and LaNiFeVMn) have been synthesized by combining five or more metallic elements to form unique body-centered cubic (BCC) or face-centered cubic (FCC) single-crystal structures exhibiting severely distorted lattices. Owing to their excellent hydrogen storage capacities, lattice distortions, high entropies, sluggish diffusions, phase and thermal stabilities, and good irradiation resistances, HEAs have emerged as excellent tritium storage alloys and promising structural materials for application to hightemperature fission or fusion nuclear devices (Table S2, Supporting Information).[5,45,46,48,140–144] The HEA distorted lattice arrangement provides increased interstitial sites to accommodate $\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{T}$ atoms, slow He diffusion, and enhance He retention. Moreover, because HEAs exhibit flexible element selectivity and element proportion controllability, they provide great space and potential for preparing different tritium/hydrogen storage alloys such as CoFeMnTiVZr (Figure 6a),[145] TiZrNbTa (Figure 6b),[143] LaNiFeVMn,[146] TiZrVMoNb,[147] TiZrMnCrFeNi[144] (Figure 6c) and TiZrVCrFeNi[148] exhibiting comprehensive properties to satisfy specific hydrogen storage requirements. Kunce et al. used
ZrTiVCrFeNi to achieve a maximum hydrogen storage capacity $(\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{M})$ of $1.81~\mathrm{wt\%}$ .[148] Sahlberg et al. used TiVZrNbHf to achieve a maximum $\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{M}$ of $2.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , which exceeded the maximum transition-metal hydride $\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{M}$ of 2 because the lattice strain favored hydrogen absorption at tetrahedral and octahedral interstitial sites (Figure 6d).[149] Shen et al. designed a series of TiZrHfMoNb HEAs containing different Mo concentrations as candidate materials for application to hydrogen/tritium storage by anticipating that a mixture of HCP-structured Ti, Zr, and Hf metals and BCC-structured Mo and Nb metals would regulate the HEA thermal stability and cell volume. Owing to their reversible single-phase transformation during hydrogen absorption–desorption cycles, all the TiZrHfMoNb alloys exhibited superior hydrogenation $(1.54~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and He retention compared to other hydrogen storage materials, which would be beneficial to improving the hydrogen recycling rate and preventing disproportionation.[150] Also, it has been shown that the bonding distance $(\mathrm{d}{<}\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{H}{>})$ variation of BCC phase was higher than that of FCC phase in TiZrHfMoNb during hydrogenation which is the vital parameter responsible for phase transformation (Figure 6e,f).[151]
Additionally, Xu et al. reported that owing to its singlecrystalline structure and high hydrogen trapping site density, FCC CoCrFeMnNi exhibited higher hydrogen isotope retention than reduced activation ferritic/martensiticCLF-1 steel.[152] Recently, Zhang et al. used $\mathrm{Ti}_{0.20}\mathrm{Zr}_{0.20}\mathrm{Hf}_{0.20}\mathrm{Nb}_{0.40}$ and reported that the maximum equilibrium hydrogen storage capacity was $1.5\:\mathrm{H}$ atom−1 at 573 K.[44] These studies suggest that HEAs have potential for application to tritium storage materials. The HEA composition complexity makes HEA tritium storage properties more adjustable to satisfy different material requirements. Several studies have shown that HEAs may exhibit good comprehensive properties such as high tritium storage capacities, recycling reversibility, He retention, and good thermodynamic properties. Such tritium storage materials can be developed based on new material design concepts and by elucidating the material helium growth mechanism. HEA tritium storage materials have been proposed based on a completely new concept and may accelerate the development of next-generation tritium storage materials.
## 4. Tritium $(\overline{{\mathsf{T}}}_{2})$ Storage and Separation Using Porous Materials
Quantum sieving (QS)-based hydrogen isotope separation using microporous materials with confined pores is a promising alternative to conventional separation methods. In porous materials, hydrogen isotopes can be efficiently separated with high selectivity in small pore apertures. This is because heavier isotopes with lower zero-point energies (ZPEs)—the minimum energy that a quantum particle exhibits (even at 0 K)—diffuse faster under cryogenic conditions or by CAQS at strong adsorption sites where heavier isotopes with lower ZPEs interact more strongly. The different isotope molecular masses lead to different isotope adsorption enthalpies $(\Delta H)$ , which predominantly results in heavier isotope adsorption.[9,12,16,60,69,77,153] To help elucidate the QS mechanism, the following section briefly discusses the fundamentals of isotope separation using porous materials.
## 4.1. Fundamental of Isotope Separation in Porous Materials
## 4.1.1. Kinetic Quantum Sieving (KQS)
In 1995, Beenakker et al. suggested a novel concept of exploiting the quantum effect, in which a heavier isotope would diffuse in a confined space faster than a lighter isotope when the pore diameter is comparable to the de Broglie wavelength of the lighter one, because the lighter isotope would create a larger energy barrier at cryogenic temperatures.[154] This quantum effect is particularly prominent in cryogenic conditions, where the difference of de Broglie wavelengths is larger. In porous materials with pores in the nanometer and subnanometer region, when the pore diameter $(d)$ is not much larger than the hard core $(\sigma)$ of the gas molecules, the transverse motion has to be quantized.[154] The entry of the molecules is restricted in its transverse motion in a space given by $d-\sigma$ . This quantity can be rather small and become comparable to the de Broglie wavelength of the molecules, which can be written as (Equation 5):
$$
\lambda\:=\:d-\sigma
$$
The quantum effect on molecular adsorption in a confined system can be explained by the ZPE of the free gas molecule and the potential depth of the adsorbed gas molecule. Beenakker et al. assumed molecules with a hard-core diameter $(\sigma)$ and approximated the interaction potential for the radial motion by a circular square well with the depth ϵ with respect to ZPE of the molecule in free space (Figure 7a–i).[154] For a circular square well in a cylinder, the energy levels can be expressed as follows (Equation 6):
$$
E_{i}=\frac{2r_{i}^{2}\hbar^{2}}{\left(d-\sigma\right)^{2}m},\mathrm{with}i=0,1,2
$$
where $E_{i}$ is counted with respect to the well bottom, $r_{i}$ is simply related to the zeros of Bessel functions, $\hbar$ is a reduced Plank constant, $\sigma$ is the molecular kinetic diameter, and $m$ is the particle mass.[154] From Equation (2), it is evident that the $E_{0}$ of a molecule in a narrow pore is inversely proportional to the de Broglie wavelength (𝜆) and the atomic mass $(m)$ of the isotope, meaning that the $E_{0}$ increases with a decreasing $d$ . Figure 7a–ii shows the behavior of $E_{0}$ of a molecule and the well depth, $\epsilon$ , as functions of the channel diameter, $d$ . Figure $7\mathrm{a}$ –ii shows four regions. In region 1, gas molecules do not exist at the pore as the pore size is smaller than that of the gas molecules.[16,154] In region 2, adsorption of gas molecules at the surface of the pore is lacking because of the existence of steric repulsion even though the pore size is slightly larger than the size of the gas molecule.[16,154] In region 4, the penetration of gas molecules is feasible, as the potential well depth is larger than the ZPE $(\epsilon>E_{0})$ . In region 3, the ZPE of gas molecules overcompensates the interaction potential $(E_{0}\ >\ \epsilon)$ at a pore larger than a molecule resulting in the quantization of the transverse motion of gas molecules in the pore.[16,154] In region 3, this barrier is strongly influenced by the different ZPEs of molecules, which are caused by the mass difference between their isotopes (see region 3 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{D}_{2}$ ).[16,154] The effect under discussion, when the adsorption in porous media is dominated by the value of the ZPE (depending on $d-\sigma$ ), can be called quantum sieving, in contrast to the well-known classical form of molecular sieving. Hence, as the aperture size approaches the gas molecule size, the ZPE of the isotope gas becomes larger than the adsorption potential energy, resulting in an increase in the penetration barrier, leading to lower equilibrium densities of the isotopes in the channel. Consequently, as heavy isotopes have a lower ZPE than light isotopes, the equilibrium adsorption amount of heavy isotope is larger than that of light isotopes under the same adsorption condition. According to this effect, heavy isotopes diffuse faster through a confined porous material than lighter isotopes, resulting in effective isotope separation under the given condition. This is the socalled kinetic quantum sieving (KQS).[16,154] More importantly, at a sufficiently low temperature (at which larger differences exist between the quantized energy levels of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ adsorbed in the nanopore), the molecular diffusivity of the isotopes will differ inside the porous materials, resulting in the separation of the isotopes.
This description proposed by Beenakker et al. can be applied to separate hydrogen isotopes via KQS in porous materials that contain ultrafine pore apertures similar to the kinetic diameter of the hydrogen isotopes under cryogenic conditions. However, it is challenging to design materials with suitably narrow pores for KQS that also show good adsorption capacities and operate at practical temperatures. Owing to their small pore sizes, several microporous materials have been investigated for KQS of hydrogen isotopes, including porous carbons,[155] zeolites,[15,33,53–55] MOFs,[9,14,61,62] COFs,[65] and organic cages.[69]
Based on KQS, many researchers have tried to examine the mechanism and relationship between the pore diameter and separation efficiency. Initially, Oh et al. experimentally investigated the quantum confinement effects of four different ultramicroporous frameworks of ZIF-7, ZIF-8, COF-1, and COF-102 whose pore apertures were $3.0\textup{\AA},3.4\textup{\AA},9.0\textup{\AA}$ , and $12\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , respectively, by cryogenic (below 77 K) hydrogen and deuterium adsorption measurements and reported that the optimum pore aperture for quantum cryo-sieving should lie between $3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $3.4\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ , which is an important guideline in the area of separating $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ isotopes via quantum cryo-sieving for designing optimized MOFs.[156] In 2019, Mondal et al. experimentally studied the KQS of hydrogen isotopes in isostructural hexagonal metal-organic frameworks having 1-D channels, named IFP-1, $-3,-4$ , and $^{-7}$ with aperture diameters of 4.2, 3.1, 1.7 and $2.1\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , respectively. Among the tested materials, IFB-4 whose pore aperture is smaller than the kinetic diameter of hydrogen, exhibited high selectivity of isotope separation by KQS.[157] Si et al. experimentally separated and purified deuterium from the hydrogen isotope mixture using MMOF-74 ( $\mathrm{\DeltaM=Co}$ , Ni, Mg, and $\mathrm{zn}^{\prime}$ ), featuring large, hexagonal, 1D channels with a diameter of $11\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , and reported that hydrogen isotope molecules enter the pores smoothly without the presence of diffusion barriers.[17] While the researchers are more inclined to utilize microporous MOFs for $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation because of their spatial confinement within small pores, Li et al. explored the advantages of microporous MOFs as ideal candidates for hydrogen isotope separation, using MOF material possessing honeycomb channels with pore sizes ranging from 1.0 to $2.6\mathrm{nm}$ including more accessible MOFs. The microporous MOFs dictate the diffusion rate of the hydrogen isotopes.[158] The importance of pore size in the determination zeolite-based hydrogen isotope separation was reported by Taguchi et al.[76] who found predominant and higher adsorption of $\mathrm{D}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{over}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at 77 K using CHAtype zeolites ion-exchanged with Na which exhibit a smaller pore size than that of LTA(3A)-type zeolites at the same experimental condition. Recent hydrogen isotope separation studies on zeolites, whose pores exhibit sizes comparable to hydrogen molecules $(3.8\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , such as CHA zeolite (pore diameter: $3.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ ),[78] FJI-Y11 zeolite (pore diameter: 3.8 Å)[78,159] and Na and K-Na exchanged zeolite A (pore diameter: ${\approx}3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ to ${\approx}4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ),[160] exhibited excellent adsorption of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ through KQS.
Although the pore environments of MOFs exhibit great tunability, significant obstacles remain in the separation of hydrogen isotopes. For example, the demands of KQS operation, inducing tight confinement in rigid frameworks by precisely modulating the pore, are difficult to achieve; this leads to extremely low operating pressure or temperature, which is not suitable for practical use.[109] From this point of view, encouraging results have been found based on the inherent advantages and flexible behavior of MOFs. Dynamic MOFs, also known as flexible MOFs or soft-porous crystals, have been developed as a new subclass of MOFs.[109] In contrast to traditional rigid MOFs, reversible structural transformation is a significant feature of dynamic MOFs, which can be triggered by isotopes or by temperature, maximally enhances KQS and contributes to the highly selective separation of hydrogen isotopes.[161] For the first time, Oh et al. exploited the structural flexibility of MOFs caused by the breathing phenomenon and achieved the highest selectivity, $S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}}=\ 13.6$ , with high separation capacity of $2.9\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ through KQS using MIL-53(Al), a representative of the flexible MOF.[61] Further, Oh et al. achieved isotope-responsive breathing using MIL-53(Al) MOF, which can selectively recognize and respond to only $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ molecules based on KQS.[70] Microscopic observation of isotopic molecular transportation in flexible MOFs during the separation process under dynamic transition plays a vital role in elucidating the mechanism and designing novel KQS MOF adsorbents, which have been achieved in only few MOFs. Oh et al. were the first to carry out microscopic observation of the diffusion dynamics of KQS-assisted hydrogen isotope separation within the flexible pores of MIL-53(Al) through a quasi-electron neutron scattering (QENS) experiment.[71] Further, KQS-assisted hydrogen isotope separation has been achieved by utilizing partially fluorinated FMOFCu,[62] MUF-15-OMe,[161] and DUT-8(Ni)[72] MOFs which are flexible in nature. However, only a few studies have investigated KQS-assisted isotope separation using flexible porous materials.
For gaseous diatomic molecules, such as molecular hydrogen, six degrees of freedom (three translations, two rotations, and one vibration) contribute to the ZPE and partition function and, thus, to the enthalpy.[9,16] Once the molecule has been adsorbed on the surface through the sorption site affinity strength, almost all the degrees of freedom may be hindered except for the vibrational energy levels normal to the surface, which is strongly influenced by the ZPE. Notably, the molecular transitional motion toward the surface is a molecule–surface vibrational motion. The specific host material adsorption site strength considerably impacts the ZPE. The hydrogen isotope ZPE difference depends on the different potential energy depths of the adsorption site (Figure 7b).[12,16] The sorption site interaction strength is expressed as a potential well depth; that is, the deeper the potential well, the larger the isotope ZPE difference. Hence, heavier adsorbed isotopes exhibit a lower ZPEs, which leads to different adsorption enthalpies and enables heavy isotopes to predominantly adsorb. Heavier isotopes adsorbed at strong adsorption sites exhibit lower ZPE, leading to different adsorption enthalpies and predominantly CAQS-based heavy isotope adsorption. Notably, the deeper the potential well, the higher the desorption temperature. MOFs and zeolites are the most widely studied class of CAQS materials for hydrogen isotope separation whose pore size and OMSs can be flexibly adjusted allowing high hydrogen isotope selectivities to be attained. Fitzgerald et al. investigated the CAQS effect for the first time, the series M-MOF-74 $[\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Co, and Ni) having a high density of OMSs as strong adsorption sites.[58] Kim et al. reported a highly effective hydrogen isotope separation system based on MOF-74-IM- $\cdot\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ by implementing the strategy of combining KQS and CAQS for the first time.[13] To date, hydrogen isotope separation based on CAQS has been studied in different kinds of MFU-4l,[60] Hofmann-type,[63] and (Be, Mg, and Ca)-doped[64] MOFs, which have shown excellent selectivities of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- ${\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ above liquid nitrogen temperatures. Recently, metal-ion-exchanged zeolites such as $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -ZSM-5,[56] Ag(I)-ZSM-5,[25] and $\mathrm{Ag(I)}$ -exchange zeolite $\Upsilon^{[57]}$ have been investigated for their excellent potential for hydrogen ion separation based on CAQS. In the following sections (Sections 4.3 and 4.4), we highlight the current understanding of hydrogen isotope separation technologies using porous materials such as zeolites and MOFs.
## 4.1.2. Chemical Affinity Quantum Sieving (CAQS)
CAQS was developed as a highly effective hydrogen isotope separation method based on the differences between isotope ZPEs and adsorption enthalpies $(\Delta H)$ , with heavier isotopes being more readily adsorbed at the strong binding sites. CAQS is one of the most promising alternative isotope separation methods because it exhibits high isotope selectivity and is energy-efficient (e.g., it operates at temperatures above liquid nitrogen).[15,56] Furthermore, CAQS is not limited by pore size. In CAQS, molecules are adsorbed at strongly attracting host material sites, and adsorbate–adsorbent interactions lead to different bond strengths and, therefore, different ZPEs.[12] This results in different adsorption enthalpies $(\Delta H)$ , which separate isotopes. Because heavier isotopes exhibit lower ZPEs, material interaction sites predominantly adsorb heavy hydrogen isotopes.
## 4.2. Hydrogen Isotope Separation in Zeolites
Zeolites are highly crystalline inorganic microporous aluminosilicate materials possessing well-defined channels, extraframework cations, uniform pore structures consisting of narrow (e.g., angstrom-range) pore diameters in addition to radiation stability. Owing to their unprecedented structural tenability and extremely complex framework topologies, zeolites are one of the promising materials to separate and store hydrogen isotopes (Table S3, Supporting Information).[20,25,53,56,57,77,78,162–164]
## 4.2.1. Hydrogen Isotope Separation via KQS and CAQS in Zeolites
Because hydrogen isotope mass ratios, which lead to different molecular motions, are much higher than those of any other isotope, Stéphanie-Victoire et al. initially used microgravimetry from 120 to $77~\mathrm{K}$ to study $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , HD, and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ adsorption on NaA zeolites and reported that the adsorption increased with increasing isotope mass.[53] Furthermore, Kawamura et al. studied the cryosorption of hydrogen isotopes at 77 K using molecular sieve 5A (MS 5A), molecular sieve 4A (MS 4A), and activated carbon and reported that MS 4A exhibited the strongest $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ adsorption among the tested materials.[165] To effectively separate hydrogen isotopes under cryogenic conditions (77.4 K), Kotoh et al. developed a pressure swing adsorption (PSA) system in which tritium was treated as gaseous hydrogen.[166] They carried out fundamental experiments of adsorption and desorption of a tracer $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ in bulk $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with Zeolite 5A-/Zeolite 13X-packed columns to separate hydrogen isotopes. The hydrogen isotope adsorption experiments on 5A and 13X zeolites exhibited quantitative advantages in the order $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ , DT, $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , HT, HD, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and the isotope adsorption difference depended on the hydrogen isotope ZPE difference at 77 K.[167] The experiments revealed that heavier isotopes predominantly adsorbed at the liquid nitrogen temperature. Also, the authors have reported that the synthetic zeolite SZ-13X was more advantageous than SZ-5A to separate hydrogen isotopes for use in PSA system. Consequently, the occurrence of QS in zeolite was reported by Kotoh et al. based on the dependence of sieve’s mesh size on temperature using molecular sieve function in zeolite-3A-crystalline lattice structure.[168] The authors verified the isotopic effect through series of adsorption– desorption experiments of $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ mixture with a zeolite 3A operated at intentionally selected temperatures. Zeolite 3A exhibited a high $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}$ separation factor of 4.33 at $130\mathrm{~K~}$ to isolate hydrogen isotopes and reported sieving above $77.4\mathrm{K}$ , which was attributed to the temperature-dependent sieve mesh size. The authors concluded that the temperature-dependent sieving is very important because different-sized hydrogen isotopes (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ ) do not adsorb at the same temperature.[168] Further, Kotoh et al. reported the significance of effective molecular diameter concerning the sieving mechanism using zeolite 3A with different molecular sieving geometry.[163] Based on the experimental results, the authors endorsed that the dynamic diameter of a molecule in rotation is a determinant for the sieving of hydrogen isotopes and the zeolite 3A having effective molecular diameter of $0.3{\mathrm{~nm}}$ was highly suitable for efficiently separating, removing, and recovering $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , HD, HT, $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , DT, and especially $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ .[163] Also, Niimura et al. observed remarkable QS in MS4A, MS5A, MS13X, RHO, and H-ZSM-11 zeolites which exhibited higher $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ separation selectivity than activated carbon fiber, carbon molecular sieves (CMS5A), and single-wall carbon nanotubes from a $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (1:1) mixture at $77~\mathrm{K}$ due to restrictive hydrogen isotope diffusion at the interconnected zeolite cylindrical pores.[164] Kowalczyk et al. studied, both experimentally and theoretically, the microscopic mechanism of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ permselective separation from $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixtures in $\mathrm{NaX}$ at 77 K.[54] The authors computed and reported the adsorption and intrinsic $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivities of $1.49\pm0.01$ and $\approx1.22\mathrm{-}1.31$ , respectively, for NaX. The authors predicted maximum adsorption selectivity at infinite dilution of adsorbed mixtures, where the most attractive adsorption centers (narrow windows interconnecting the NaX cages) are preferentially occupied. Also, the author stated that the intrinsic selectivity was favorable for $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ which was driven by adsorption selectivity (i.e., by thermodynamic equilibrium). Among different methods to separate hydrogen isotopes, Salazar et al. investigated the adsorption of $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ on faujasite $\mathrm{NaX}$ zeolite supercages (Figure 8a) based on the physisorption due to quantum effects at low temperatures (below 100 K).[77] Through manometry experiments $\left(30-150\ \mathrm{K}\right)$ and molecular dynamics simulations (40 and 77 K), the authors reported higher $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ adsorption than the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed by the zeolite at a given temperature mainly below 77 K because of an increase of the loading capacity with the isotope mass and with a decrease of temperature. The authors simulated the adsorption of $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ with that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ adsorption and reported the preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules. Further, the influence of the nature of extra framework cations on the $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption selectivity was studied by Giraudet et al.[33] The authors studied the influence of $\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{Al}$ ratio and that of pore size towards the $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption selectivity at $77.4\mathrm{~K~}$ below $1000~\mathrm{{hPa}}$ for a series of FAU type zeolites X containing $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ , $\mathtt{B a}^{2+}$ , and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ extraframework cations through coadsorption measurements realized by manometry combined with mass spectrometry analysis of the gas phase and compared the results with that of NaY, DAY, and pure CHA and MFI zeolites. Based on the experiments, they inferred that the selectivity of the pure silica zeolites remains almost constant with loading $(1.5\pm0.2)\$ despite the decrease of pore size from $11\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in DAY to $4.6\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in MFI. In contrast, the authors observed considerably higher selectivity at low loading in zeolites X. The best selectivity, equal to 5.7, is observed for $\mathrm{{\calMg}X}$ at a loading of ${\approx}11$ molec per uc. However, at higher loadings, the selectivity decreases for all studied materials to the value obtained for the cation-free (pure silica) zeolites. Such behavior is attributed to the existence of strong cation–molecule interactions at low loading which provide the largest difference in the zeropoint energies of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ and consequently the highest values of selectivity. The drop in the selectivity observed at high loading may be attributed to much weaker adsorbate zeolite interactions existing in this range.[33] Whereas, Gao et al. studied the quantum effects on dynamics for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffusion in pure silica zeolite (RHO) and found inverse kinetic isotopic sieving effect (KIE) at low temperatures which was responsible for the faster diffusion of heavier isotopic species than the lighter one.[55] Also the authors have reported that the KIE was contributed through three quantum effects such as: i) quantum mechanical tunneling; ii) zero-point energy (ZPE); and iii) thermal vibrational energy. Furthermore, Taguchi et al. reported that more $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ had adsorbed on CHA-type zeolites ion-exchanged with K, Na, or Ca at 77, 201, and $250\mathrm{K}.^{[76]}$ At higher temperatures (e.g., 201 K), the $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ uptake was higher than at 77 K, which may because the thermally expanded zeolite framework had slightly increased the pore size and had enabled $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ molecules $(0.269\ \mathrm{nm})$ —which are smaller than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules $(0.289\ \mathrm{nm})$ —to predominantly be adsorbed.[76] Due to the smaller pore size of $\mathrm{{Na}}$ -CHA, a $S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ value of 1.15 was obtained for the Na-CHA using a $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ mixed gas at $201\mathrm{~K~}$ which was larger than the value of 1.08 determined for the LTA. The CHA-type zeolites showed larger $S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ values than the LTA above 201 K. This result suggests that pore size is important for determining zeolite-based hydrogen isotope separation. By considering QKS (a diffusion-driven separation due to different energy barriers) in addition to the QS responsible for enhancing heavier-isotope adsorption, Perez-Carbajo et al. computationally screened 210 pure-silica zeolites through computational investigation and reported that zeolite BCT was a preeminent candidate for separating $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at cryogenic temperatures up to $200~\mathrm{K}$ and in the range $10^{-1}{-}10^{5}\ \mathrm{kPa}$ .[20] Zeolite BCT exhibited the highest adsorption selectivity reported for $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation at low temperatures, and deuterium adsorption was 80 or 130 times greater than hydrogen adsorption at $40~\mathrm{K},$ , depending on the pressure. Moreover, a high deuterium selectivity $(S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}})$ of 20 was achieved at $50~\mathrm{K}$ and atmospheric pressure. In $\bar{\mathrm{T}}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation, zeolite BCT exhibits extremely high selectivity, favoring heavier isotope separation. At zero loading, the BCT $\mathrm{T}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivities were 354, 8.07, and 1.43 at 25, 70, and $200~\mathrm{K}.$ , respectively (Figure 8c). Overall, the BCT-type zeolite clearly stands out for highly selective $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ separations over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and for achieving the highest reported selectivity at cryogenic temperatures. This study provides an interesting perspective for using porous materials to achieve $\mathrm{T}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation exhibiting extremely high selectivity. More importantly, Xiong et al. explored thermodynamic quantum cryo sieving based on CAQS in zeolite 5A below 77 K and achieved $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivities of 8.83, 6.42, 4.98, 3.96, and 4.04 at 30, 35, 40, 50, and $60~\mathrm{K},$ respectively, at 50 mbar (Figure 8b).[15] The increased selectivity with decreasing temperature is controlled by the thermodynamic adsorption equilibrium (i.e., CAQS) because $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ exhibit different adsorption ZPEs. Further, Bezverkhyy et al. reported $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption selectivities under cryogenic temperatures (45–77 K) in Na and K-Na exchanged zeolites A (LTA structural code).[160] The authors evidenced excellent $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity through the partial substitution of $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ by $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ in LTA zeolite. Due to the partial substitution, the $8\mathrm{MR}$ windows of Na-LTA zeolites are partially blocked by $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ cations which enabled the accessibility only to $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ molecules through a fraction of $\alpha$ -cages. Due to this effect, $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivities of 23.0 and 8.0 were obtained using $\mathrm{K}_{1.6}\mathrm{Na}_{10.4}\mathrm{A}$ and NaA, respectively.[160] Because molecules in pores exhibiting sizes comparable to hydrogen molecules (3.8 Å) exhibit different ZPEs, Bezverkhyy et al. used QS to separate hydrogen isotopes in a CHA zeolite framework exhibiting an ${\approx}3.8\mathring\mathrm{A}$ aperture.[78] At a higher loading of $12{\mathrm{mmol}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and smallpore, high-cation-content, Al-rich sodium-exchanged chabazites $(\mathrm{Na}_{3.9}\mathrm{Al}_{3.9}\mathrm{Si}_{8.1}\mathrm{O}_{24})$ (Na-CHA) exhibited a high selectivity of 13 at 40 K (Figure 8d); this was superior to the zeolite 5A selectivity (5 at $40\mathrm{K}$ and $50\mathrm{hPa}^{[15]}$ ) and MOF selectivities (13.6 at $40\mathrm{K}$ (at a loading of $2.9\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) for MIL-53(Al)[61] and 12 at $60~\mathrm{K}$ for MOF$74(\mathrm{Co})^{[9]})$ . The cations at these sites enable adsorption properties to be adjusted by varying the Si/Al ratio and the cation itself. These results indicate that Na-CHA exhibited higher selectivity and adsorption at higher loadings in contrast to MOFs, which exhibited high selectivity at lower loadings and suggest that smallpore zeolites are crucial for industrial applications requiring high loadings. Ion-exchanged zeolites are considered promising materials for the separation of hydrogen isotopes. Until now, hydrogen isotope separation abilities of many ion-exchanged zeolites have been tested successfully. Xiong et al. separated hydrogen isotopes based on the strong chemical affinity between hydrogen isotopes $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ , $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ ) and zeolite active metal sites through dihydrogen bonds.[56] The authors developed a $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ ZSM-5 zeolite (Figure 8e) exhibiting excellent thermal stability toward hydrogen isotope separation and high selectivity in a technologically favorable range from the liquid nitrogen temperature to approximately room temperature through dihydrogen bonding between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ isotopes and active metal-ion-exchanged $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ centers. Thermal desorption spectroscopy measurements revealed that the $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ -ZSM-5 zeolite exposed to 1:1 $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ gas mixtures exhibited a $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 24.9, which is the highest selectivity ever measured for $\mathrm{Cu(I)}{\cdot}\mathrm{ZSM}{\cdot}5$ at $100\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 8f). This may be attributed to strong chemical affinity through dihydrogen bonds on $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ sites exhibiting a large isotope effect due to $\Delta E_{\mathrm{ZPE}}$ and $\Delta H$ . The result clearly demonstrates that $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -exchanged zeolites are promising low-cost materials for enriching and separating $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ and ${\sf D}_{2}$ from hydrogen isotope mixtures in a technologically favorable range from the liquid nitrogen temperature to approximately room temperature. Because $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ ions are unstable in the ZSM-5 zeolite during hydrogen separation, Xiong et al. used a strong-CAQS-based $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ -exchanged ZSM-5(Ag(I)-ZSM5) zeolite (Figure 8g) and subsequently reported $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivities of 8.7 and 3.1 at 77 and $160~\mathrm{K}$ respectively.[25] During the temperature swing, an isotope mixture containing $2.5\%$ $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ was enriched to a $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ concentration of $95.1\%$ . More importantly, the authors predicted a $\mathrm{T}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ selectivity of 3.7 at 77 K (Figure 8h), indicating that Ag(I)-ZSM-5 may be used to enrich tritium from a tritium–deuterium mixture—which is one of the most important tritium cycling steps while generating nuclear fusion energy. Structural analysis and density functional theory (DFT) calculations revealed that the high $\mathrm{Ag(I)}$ -ZSM-5 zeolite hydrogen isotope selectivity could be attributed to the strong chemical affinity between the hydrogen isotopes and the active $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ sites. When Ni, $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ , and $\mathrm{Ag(I)}$ active sites were incorporated into $\mathrm{{Ag}(I)}$ -ZSM5, the hydrogen isotope separation temperature and selectivity both considerably increased. Owing to its facile industrial synthesis and stability, the Ag(I)-ZSM-5 sorbent offers potential practical solutions to the challenging problems of separating and enriching hydrogen isotopes, especially tritium. Nevertheless, the adsorption capacity is limited by the low density of exchanged ions in ZSM-5 which was not favorable for practical applications. Further, based on previous studies, Hirscher et al. demonstrated the significance of substantial density of strong adsorption sites in an ideal zeolite towards the separation of hydrogen isotopes which can preferentially attract $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ even at high temperatures.[57] Through systematic thermal desorption spectroscopic (TDS) investigation, Hirscher et al. reported a high $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 10 at $90~\mathrm{K}$ for 1:1 isotope mixture using $\mathrm{Ag(I)}$ -exchanged zeolite Y via CAQS. The authors stated that the high Al content of the zeolite structure leads to a high density of Ag sites and the uncoordinated ${\tt A g}^{+}$ acts as a strong adsorption site preferentially adsorbing approximately one-third of the heavier isotopologue in the zeolite framework even above liquid nitrogen temperature.[57]
## 4.3. Hydrogen Isotope Separation in MOFs
Owing to their ultrahigh porosities, large specific surface areas, and tunable pore structures, nanoporous MOFs are excellent candidates for application to hydrogen isotopologue QS (Table S4, Supporting Information).[8,9] Moreover, the MOF crystalline framework enables the aperture size and functionality (i.e., OMSs, which act as strong adsorption sites) to be precisely controlled by accurately designing molecular structures, which makes MOFs very attractive for storing gas by physisorption on MOF inner surfaces.[9,12] Some MOFs exhibit unique structural flexibility, which clearly distinguishes them from other inorganic porous materials such as zeolites and porous silica.[61,72,169]
## 4.3.1. Hydrogen Isotope Separation via KQS and CAQS in MOFs
Experiments by Chen et al. unequivocally demonstrated $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ kinetic isotope quantum molecular sieving in a zinc/copper MOF for the first time.[170] Further, Noguchi et al. directly evidenced the quantum molecular sieving effect for adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ CuBOTf.[171] The authors used CuBOTf MOFs to experimentally measure $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}$ selectivities of 1.2 and in the range 2.6–5.6 at 77 and $40\mathrm{K},$ respectively, for a 1:1 $\mathrm{H}_{2}{-}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ mixed gas. The higher selectivity at $40\mathrm{~K~}$ may be because quantum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ exhibited more prominent pore blocking than quantum $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , which explicitly exhibited QS in MOFs. In addition, Oh et al. experimentally investigated the feasibility and quantum sieving effect of various microporous frameworks (MOFs, COFs, and ZIFs) towards hydrogen isotope separation and found that the molar ratio $(n\mathrm{D}_{2}/n\mathrm{H}_{2})$ is strongly dependent on the pore size, temperature, and pressure.[156] Under optimum pressure and low temperature, the materials with sufficiently narrow pore size (exhibiting confinement effects) favored the preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{\ddot{D}}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . More importantly, the authors found that the optimum aperture diameter for quantum cryosieving should be between 3.0 and $3.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , which are important guidelines for designing optimized organic frameworks for separating hydrogen isotopes via quantum cryo-sieving.
As an alternative to kinetic isotope QS, hydrogen isotopologue mixtures (i.e., $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2})$ ) may be separated using thermodynamic effects through the incorporation of metal sites in MOF frameworks via CAQS. The separation is predominantly attributed to the difference in the zero-point energies of the isotopologues when they are adsorbed at the metal site. FitzGerald et al. first explored CAQS in QS-based MOFs between $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ for an Fe-, Co-, and Ni-MOF-74 MOF series over the range 77–150 K.[58] They reported that Ni-MOF-74 exhibited a higher isosteric adsorption heat for $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ versus $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which was primarily attributed to the different ZPEs of the isotopologues adsorbed at the metal sites. The $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity calculated by applying the IAS theory at 77, 87, 100, 120, and $150\mathrm{~K~}$ increased from 1.5 to 5.0 at 150 and 77 K, respectively (Figure 9a). The $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (1:1) mixture exhibited the maximum selectivity of 11.8 at $60~\mathrm{K}$ and 30 mbar. A high selectivity of ${\approx}6.3$ was even achieved at $80~\mathrm{K}$ and 30 mbar, which is well above the liquid nitrogen temperature and indicates the importance of CAQS in hydrogen isotope separation.[58] Further, Oh et al. incorporated numerous unsaturated metal coordination sites in CPO-27 family (also referred to as MOF-74) and greatly enhanced $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation well above the liquid nitrogen temperature because of CAQS.[9] To test the role of OMSs, the authors compared the $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molar ratio of CPO-27-Co to a covalent organic framework (COF-1), possessing similar pore size $(9\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ and no OMSs (Figure 9b). In contrast to CPO-27-Co, the molar ratio of COF-1 increases with decreasing temperature which concluded the role of OMSs in the determination of selectivity–temperature relation of the framework. The authors used a CPO-27-Co MOF to achieve an $S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ of ${\approx}11.8\pm1.3$ at 30 mbar and $60~\mathrm{K}$ by exploiting QS and different adsorbed isotope ZPEs. The strong attraction between the unsaturated ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ centers and hydrogen molecules keeps these sites occupied at relatively high temperatures, and the isotope effects are strong enough to change the enthalpy over a wide temperature range suitable for industrial applications.[9] In contrast to the appreciable CAQS-induced hydrogen isotope selectivity in CPO-27 exhibiting OMSs, Savchenko et al. reported an appreciable hydrogen isotopologues selectivity of 1.7 at $50\mathrm{K}$ in MFU4l (Figure 9c), a framework that neither shows strongly attractive OMSs for adsorption nor gates that could act as a quantum sieve.[59] The TDS (Figure 9d) of MFU-4l shows significant peak at lower desorption temperature with the presence of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ signal at $50\mathrm{K}$ and above. The MFU-4l selectivity was based on the small difference between the ZPE of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ adsorbed at $\approx50~\mathrm{K}$ . The results showed that although sizeable CAQS can be achieved in typical large-surface-area MOFs, the operational temperature required for effectively separating hydrogen isotopologues is considerably lower than those of frameworks exhibiting OMSs, such as CPO-27. Subsequently, Weinrauch et al. improved the hydrogen isotope separation efficiency of MFU-4l by enhancing the adsorption enthalpy of local sites.[60] By replacing terminal corner $Z\mathrm{n-Cl}$ sites of MFU-4l with $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ metal sites exhibiting high hydrogen adsorption enthalpy, the authors achieved pronounced isotope effects with higher separation factors with the shifting of adsorption/desorption window to higher temperature, even at temperatures well above $100~\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 9e).[60] At the strongly binding $(32\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ) $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ sites, nuclear quantum effects cause heavier isotopes to exhibit higher adsorption enthalpies, which more efficiently aid predominantly heavier isotope adsorption above $80~\mathrm{K}.$ The authors reported the highest $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}$ selectivity of 11 at $100~\mathrm{K}$ to date, which is higher than $S=12$ at $60~\mathrm{K}$ for CPO-27(Co),[9] owing to the large $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{D}}_{2}\mathrm{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy difference of $2.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . Thus, elucidating a strong isotope exchange mechanism at and above $90~\mathrm{K}$ would be an important research outcome. At lower temperatures, adsorption is kinetically controlled, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ diffuses into the MOF and occupies the $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ adsorption site on a first-come-first-serve basis. At a higher temperature of approximately $90~\mathrm{{K}}$ , $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ more strongly adsorbs by replacing $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ through an isotope-exchange-induced thermodynamically controlled mechanism. Evidently, $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ - MFU-4l is promising for selecting and purifying HD and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ from an isotope mixture and enables the selective capture of tritium $(\mathrm{T}_{2})$ and DT with high efficiency. Owing to its high selectivity, MFU-4l may be a promising candidate for producing high-purity deuterium and capturing tritium from low-concentration hydrogen isotope mixtures. Furthermore, applications can be scaled by increasing the strong adsorption site density. For the first time, Kim et al. reported highly effective hydrogen isotope separation system based on MOF by implementing the strategy of combining KQS and CAQS.[13] For this purpose, MOF-74-Ni providing CAQS functionality owing to its hydrogen adsorption enthalpies at OMSs was chosen as the host material. KQS functionality, on the other hand, was provided through a post-modification strategy to introduce imidazole molecules (IMs) to the 1-D MOF-74-Ni channel (Figure 10a) as a diffusion barrier, which effectively reduced the aperture size and repeatedly blocked lighter-isotope diffusion. By optimizing the number of coordinated imidazole molecules via a post-synthetic strategy, the authors prepared “MOF-74-IM-10,” “MOF-74-IM-38,” and “MOF-74-IM-70,” samples wherein $10\%$ , $38\%$ , or $70\%$ of all the $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ OMSs were occupied by imidazole molecules, respectively, whose overall selectivities $(S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}})$ and the corresponding adsorbed $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ concentrations are presented in Figure 10b. The authors maximized CAQS and KQS synergy to develop a highly effective MOF-based isotope separation system exhibiting a high $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ uptake $\left(2.8\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}\right.\$ ) and the highest reported separation factor as high as ${\approx}26$ at 77 K. Combining KQS and CAQS will provide opportunities for intelligently designing porous materials to adsorb tritium and deuterium and separate hydrogen and helium mixtures. Although combining KQS and CAQS enhanced the $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity (as previously reported), QS at a low operational temperature of $20~\mathrm{K}$ remains challenging for industrial applications. To improve low-temperature QS, Cao et al. screened $\{{\mathrm{[Fe(OH)(H_{2}b t a)](H_{2}O)}}\}_{n}$ MOFs exhibiting regular 1-D ultramicropores and achieved an extraordinary separation factor as high as $41.4\pm0.4$ at $20\mathrm{K}$ , which currently represents state-of-the-art QS-based hydrogen isotope separation.[108] The results of this work provide a more explicit direction for designing MOF materials toward QS hydrogen isotopes for industrial separation applications. Therefore, attention should be paid to both OMSs and ultramicropore apertures when designing MOF materials. Meanwhile, FitzGerald et al. investigated hydrogen isotope separation within $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -MFU-4l MOF using combination of breakthrough analysis, equilibrium measurements, and infrared spectroscopy and achieved a $\mathrm{D}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{over}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 11.1 at $100\mathrm{K}.$ . By 77 K, the kinetic effects reduced the selectivity to 1 (i.e., no selectivity).[172] Through static and dynamic measurements, the authors also evidenced that the material’s selectivity for $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ increases with decreasing temperature, reaching a maximum at ${\approx}100\mathrm{~K~}$ , but then decreases for lower temperatures. Thus, the authors concluded that the efficacy of such materials is kinetically limited at lower temperatures, even though the strongly binding materials may produce large differences in $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ versus $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ZPEs (and, thus, high selectivity). Han et al. used a screening method to efficiently identify optimal candidates capable of separating hydrogen isotopes from the vast number of existing MOFs.[14] The authors screed out SIFSIX-3-Zn MOF by QS in consideration of the synergistic effect of aperture size, ZPE, and pore structure, which exhibited the highest selectivity (53.8 at $25\mathrm{\kPa}$ ) ever reported for a 1:1 $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixture at $20~\mathrm{K}$ . The results were confirmed using advanced cryogenic thermal desorption spectroscopy (ACTDS) and indicate that SIFSIX-3-Zn is a promising hydrogen separation MOF candidate. The SIFSIX-3- Zn MOF (Figure 10c) exhibited a selectivity that not only was considerably higher than that achieved using commercial cryogenic distillation $(S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}}=3.0$ at 20 K) but also was far superior compared to those previously reported for any adsorbent. SIFSIX-3- $Z\mathrm{n}$ predominantly exhibited enhanced $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ adsorption compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption because the 1-D channel exhibited an ideal aperture of 3.86 Å and because the $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ΔZPE is approximately
$1.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , which hinders $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption. Consequently, $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ was predominantly adsorbed compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . More importantly, the ultramicrochannels exhibited a relatively high affinity for guest molecules, and the abundant $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ fluorine atoms could also strengthen the interaction between $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ molecules and the framework through hydrogen bonding. Owing to the structure and interaction synergy, this MOF exhibited excellent separation performance for the $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixture at $20\mathrm{K}$ . These results may provide useful information for screening MOFs for other adsorptive separation targets and not only demonstrate that SIFSIX-3-Zn is a promising candidate for application to highly effective hydrogen isotope separation but also will contribute to the development of an efficient method for screening MOFs for specific adsorptive separation targets. Using an indigenously designed dynamic column breakthrough device (e.g., a cryogenic apparatus equipped with a mass spectrometer detector), Si et al. analyzed the abilities of M-MOF-74 $[\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co}$ , Ni, $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ , or Zn) exhibiting an ${\approx}11\mathrm{\AA}$ hexagonal channel and a high OMS density to separate $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ from hydrogen isotope mixtures and purify the separated $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ .[17] Owing to its lower ZPE and higher adsorption enthalpy, much more $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ was predominantly adsorbed in M-MOF-74 versus $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ under the same conditions. More importantly, the Ni-, Co-, $\mathrm{Mg\mathrm{-}}$ , and Zn-MOF-74 selectivities $\left(\mathrm{nD}_{2}/\mathrm{nH}_{2}\right)$ ) (Figure 10d) were approximately 4.8, 3.5, 3.5, and 1.7 at 77 K, respectively, which are much higher than those achieved using other materials such as porous carbon, zeolite, and COFs. The theoretical simulations and experimental breakthrough data clearly showed that M-MOF-74 $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{M}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{o}}$ , Ni, or $\lvert\mathrm{ug}\rvert$ breakthrough depends on not only strong binding sites but also the hydrogen isotope adsorption capacity and OMS density. Because breakthrough gas separation is inexpensive and consumes little energy, it has potential for broad application to hydrogen isotope separation and could provide a direction for synthesizing next-generation hydrogen isotope separation materials. Furthermore, the authors used a chemically, thermally, and aqueously stable 3D microporous Cu-based MOF (FJI-Y11) exhibiting a CHA topology to adsorb hydrogen and deuterium through a single ligand. They demonstrated that the carboxyl group and ligand-methylene-connected oxygen atoms played a major role as priority hydrogen and deuterium adsorption sites instead of the exposed Cu OMSs on the paddlewheel clusters. Because of the suitable pore space and strong binding sites, FJI-Y11 exhibited a selectivity of 1.76 at $77\mathrm{K}$ and $100\mathrm{kPa}$ , which is higher than those of HKUST-1 (1.42) and zeolite 5A, which exhibits a similar pore size. More importantly, dynamic breakthrough experiments using highly water stable FJI-Y11 at 77 K and 1 bar revealed that the $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ separation factor reached 1.4 for the $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}\left(10/10/80\mathrm{vol}\%\right)$ mixed gas. Therefore, FJIY11 is a promising candidate material for industrially separating and storing heavier hydrogen isotopes such as deuterium and tritium and provides a strategy for synthesizing next-generation hydrogen isotope separation materials.[159] While the chemists are more inclined to use microporous MOFs for $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation due to their spatial confinement effects within the small pores, Li et al. reported the advantages of mesoporous MOFs over microporous MOFs toward $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation. Li et al. researched the effect of pore size on $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation through dynamic column breakthrough experiments using MOF-74 analogs with abundant OMSs and honeycomb 1D channels with pore sizes ranging between 1.0 and $2.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ .[158] The authors reported higher $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation using mesoporous MOF-74 than that of microporous one by taking CAQS effect into consideration which may be attributed to the more accessible OMSs and higher diffusion rate of hydrogen isotope. It has been previously reported that MOFs possessing a high density of OMSs resulted in excellent hydrogen isotope separation via CAQS.[9,13,58] As a first attempt, Ha et al. realized both high $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ capacity and high selectivity in the same system using Hofmann-type MOFs having extraordinarily high density of OMSs.[63] The authors investigated Hofmann-type MOFs, $\mathrm{Co(pyz)[M(CN)_{4}]}$ (pyz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=$ pyrazine, $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{P d}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ ), which have micropores of dimensions $4.0\times3.9\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ and an extraordinarily high density of OMSs $(\approx9~\mathrm{mmol~cm^{-3}})$ ), to achieve hydrogen isotope separation through CAQS effect. Owing to the preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{D}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{over}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at strongly binding OMSs, the Hofmann-type MOF, $\mathrm{Co(pyz)[Pd(CN)_{4}]}$ exhibited a high selectivity $(S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}})$ of 21.7 as well as a large $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ uptake of 10 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $25~\mathrm{K}$ . Recently, Dong et al. studied the hydrogen isotope separation ability of the alkali earth metals (Be, $\mathsf{{M g}}$ , and Ca)- doped UiO-67 MOFs by considering KQS (based on pore size) and CAQS (based on adsorption sites) effects.[64] Through DFT calculations based on ZPE and adsorption enthalpy, the authors reported better $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ separation performance for Be-doped UiO69 than other alkali earth metal dopants and unsaturated metal sites in MOFs based on CAQS. Moreover, the $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 49.4 at 77 K was predicted for Be-doped UiO-69. Diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform (DRIFT) spectroscopy is an excellent tool to simultaneously monitor the adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ at the same metal site, and to directly observe the adsorptive separation behavior.[173] For the first time, Yan et al. developed in situ DRIFT spectroscopy to observe the hydrogen isotope separation behavior at active Cu1 sites within Cu1-MFU-4l. The Cu1-MFU-4l MOF exhibited preferential adsorption of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ - over- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with high selectivity $(S_{\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}})$ of 11.1 at $100\mathrm{K}$ via CAQS, evidenced by the authors through the direct capturing of selective adsorption phenomena of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ using in situ DRIFT spectroscopy.[173] This work reveals the significance of in situ DRIFT spectroscopy to clarify the CAQS mechanisms of isotopologues at active sites within the porous adsorbents which will help in developing porous adsorbents to separate hydrogen isotopes with high efficiency.[173]
## 4.3.2. Hydrogen Isotope Separation Using Flexible MOFs
The flexible framework of MOFs can selectively respond to external stimuli adapting its pore size triggered by adsorption-induced gating or breathing and this strategy was successfully applied in the selective separation of hydrogen isotopes. For the first time, Kim et al. exploited the structural flexibility of MOFs caused by the breathing phenomenon for hydrogen isotope separation.[61] Kim et al. used the dynamic “breathing” transition of a flexible MIL-53(Al) MOF (Figure 10e) to develop an effective strategy to separate hydrogen isotopes by dynamically transitioning narrow pores (nps) into large pores (lps). During the flexible and reversible breathing transition, the pore apertures were continuously adjusted, which maximized QS and resulted in a high selectivity.[61] This technique provides a tremendous opportunity to separate mixtures of similar-sized and similar-shaped molecules that require precise pore tuning. Figure 10f shows the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ sorption isotherms generated at 20, 40, and 77 K, which clearly indicate the MIL-53(Al) flexibility. Breathing was systematically tuned by adjusting the exposure pressure and time, and the highest selectivity ( $S_{\mathrm{D}_{7}/\mathrm{H}_{7}}=13.6)$ was achieved with a high separation capacity of $2.9\:\bar{\mathrm{mmol}}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $40~\mathrm{K}$ and was strongly related to the MIL-53(Al) pore structure state.[61] This novel strategy of exploiting the dynamic pore change during the breathing of MIL-53(Al) shows the potential of the dynamic flexibility in designing efficient system for separating gas mixtures of atoms/molecules with similar size and shape with high selectivity and separation capacity. Whereas, Zhang et al. experimentally investigated a partially fluorinated MOF (FMOFCu) for efficient $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ extraction from hydrogen isotope mixture. The FMOFCu MOF consists of a trimodal pore system, featuring temperature-triggered gate opening, with large tubular cavities connected through a smaller cavity with bottleneck apertures with a size of $3.{\bar{6}}{\bar{\mathrm{\AA}}}$ plus a third hidden cavity connected by an even smaller aperture of $2.5\textup{\AA}$ . Depending on the temperature, these two apertures show a gate-opening effect, and the cavities get successively accessible for hydrogen isotopes with increasing temperature which resulted in efficient separation of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ with a selectivity of 14 and 4 at 25 and 77 K, respectively. This is a breakthrough, since hardly any MOFs exhibit such a high sorption temperature based on KQS.[62] Developing a novel responsive system capable of molecular recognition of physicochemically similar mixtures such as isotopes is an exciting scientific challenge, possibly ushering in a new era for smart separation materials.[70] As an improved strategy, for the first time, Kim et al. reported flexible system capable of recognizing and responding only to specific isotope molecules which can be referred to as isotope-responsive breathing.[70] Using in situ neutron diffraction experiments, the authors examined a unique isotope-responsive breathing transition of the flexible MIL-53(Al) MOF which can selectively recognize and respond to only $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ molecules. The authors concluded that the isotope-selective sorption increase is attributed to the additional second breathing transition of MIL-53(Al), rather than to the partial condensation of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ within the same pore structure of MIL-53(Al) in the first breathing step. This study opens new avenues for developing novel smart materials capable of simultaneously recognizing and responding to specific isotope molecules.[70] Further, based on the findings of Kim et al.,[70] a $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ - responsive third adsorption step using diamondoid frameworkstructured cobalt formate (CoFA) was revealed by Muhammad et al.[174] which renders a promising separation performance, with a $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity up to 44 at $25\:\mathrm{K}/1\$ bar. More importantly, CoFA makes it a prospect for commercialization as it was synthesized on a gram scale.[174] Whereas, Qazvini et al. reported quantum sieving of $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ using MUF-15-OMe which is a dynamic MOF capable of flexing in response to guest uptake with a reversible, pore-expanding structural transformation.[161] The flexing of MUF-15-OMe opens up space as one of its linkers partially detaches to allow the framework to expand and generate additional pore spaces for the adsorption of specific additional gas molecules which enables the differentiation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ by quantum sieving. This study underpins the use of dynamic MOFs for challenging separations.[161] $\mathrm{In}2013$ , Contescu et al. conducted QENS of $\mathrm{~\cal~H~}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ in polyfurfuryl alcohol-derived activated carbon (PFAC) between 10 and $40\mathrm{K}$ , which demonstrated extreme quantum sieving, with $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ diffusing up to 76 times faster than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and with high mobility, which resulted in significant $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ kinetic selectivity.[175] The authors demonstrated through QENS that the two isotopes exhibit completely distinct diffusion mechanisms caused by the different strength of quantum effects on their intermolecular interactions. Also, the authors observed that the thermal de Broglie wavelength of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ at their respective mobility onset temperatures were identical and correlated with the dominant pore size.[175] For the first time, Oh et al. carried out microscopic observation of the diffusion dynamics of KQS-assisted hydrogen isotope separation within the flexible pores of MIL-53(Al) through a QENS experiment.[71] The authors observed a large diffusivity difference between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ at low temperatures owing to the presence of a narrow pore which was similar to rigid framework structure as that of carbon molecular sieves reported by Nguyen et al.[176] using QENS. Unlike rigid structures, the authors observed that the dynamic breathing of the framework increased the diffusion difference of the isotopes as the pore structure was increased when the temperature was increased from 25 to $77~\mathrm{K}.$ Such an inverse behavior of the correlation between isotope diffusion and an increase in temperature provides the basis for the design novel flexible MOF systems utilizing KQS for isotope-separation process even at higher temperatures.[71] The experiment showed that the microscopic observation of isotopic molecular transportation in flexible MOFs during the separation process under dynamic transition helps to elucidate the mechanism and the design of novel KQS MOF adsorbents, which had been achieved only in few MOFs.[71] Whereas Yin et al. synthesized a unique 3d-5f bimetal cage-based MOF containing $\mathrm{[Th_{8}C o_{8}]}$ nanocage with $3.0\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ windows, called ECUT-8, exhibiting a combination of QS and molecular sieving (MS) characteristics and evidenced the clear separations of both $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ isotopes via breakthrough experiments.[169] The ECUT8 MOF with expandable effective aperture, due to the flexibility of imidazolate-derivative ligand, could pass molecules with kinetic diameters ${<}5.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ through “one-step” and “two-step” gate opening mechanisms which fulfilled the separation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ through QS effect. Thanks to their nearly identical physicochemical properties, the separation of water isotopologues by traditional distillation and electrolysis methods can encounter significant obstacles. Recently Su et al. provided unprecedented separation of isotopologues without significant energy expenditure by a diffusion-regulatory mechanism based on the dynamic nature of porous coordination polymers (PCPs) or MOFs, which provides flexible framework to regulate guest traffic, as well as guest recognition for separation.[30] Using Cu-based PCP, the authors demonstrated kinetic-based vapor separation of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{HDO}/\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ ternary mixtures with high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ separation factors of around 210 at 298 K. The outstanding discrimination features are attributed to “diffusion by local dynamic motion (DLDM)” through which the diffusion of water isotopologues are controlled in addition to the amplification of their rate differences by using locally dynamic frameworks to induce the efficient separation of water isotopologues. This rationale can be more broadly suitable for use with diverse adsorbents for the efficient discrimination of isotopologues.[30] More importantly, for the first time, Bondorf et al. reported the selective adsorption of protium-free dihydrogen isotopologues (responds to $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ but not to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and HD) using flexible DUT-8(Ni) MOF.[72] The pressure-dependent phase transition of DUT-8(Ni) MOF resulted in an outstanding $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over$\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 11.6 at $23.3~\mathrm{K}.$ , with high D2 uptake. From firstprinciples calculations coupled with statistical thermodynamics, the authors predicted that the isotope-selective gate opening in DUT-8(Ni) MOF might be originated from the pronounced nuclear quantum effects (NQEs) of the adsorbed dihydrogen phase. Also, the simulations suggest DUT-8(Ni) to remain closed in the presence of HT, while it also opens for DT and $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ , demonstrating gate opening as a highly effective approach for isotopologue separation and offers a path to highly purified heavy hydrogen.[72]
## 5. Tritium $(\overline{{\mathsf{I}}}_{2})$ Storage and Separation Using 2-D Layered Materials
Atomically thick 2-D layered materials featuring unique nanopores and/or nanochannels/interlayer galleries (Figure 11) exhibit fascinating properties and have emerged as highperformance membrane materials for storing and separating hydrogen isotopes.[81] Layered 2D materials such as graphene, h-BN, and MXenes exist as single layers or bulk-stacked multilayers separated by interstitial spaces such that the adjacent monolayer planes are connected by weak van der Waals (vdW) interactions.[80,177] In layered 2-D materials, hydrogen isotopes are separated by sieving through either the nanopores or the interstitial space between the atomic planes of the layers providing angstrom-range channels, which aid quantum confinement. Whereas hydrogen isotopes are stored by defects, voids, and encapsulation between adjacent layers.
The interstitial space (e.g., vdW gap) between layered crystal atomic planes provides angstrom-range channels that quantum confine hydrogen isotopes (e.g., sieving-induced hydrogen isotope separation) because the isotopes exhibited different de Broglie wavelengths, even at room temperature.[80,84,85,178] The energy barrier difference required for hydrogen isotopes to penetrate the layered material interstitial space was the isotope separation driving factor.[85] In 2014, Hu et al. determined thermal proton permeabilities in monolayer graphene and h-BN.[79] $\mathrm{In}2016$ , the same group found that monolayer graphene and h-BN not only allowed protons to transfer through but also could sieve hydrogen isotopes [e.g., protons $\left(\mathrm{H^{+}}\right)$ and deuterons $\left(\mathsf{D}^{+}\right)]$ at room temperature while exhibiting a high $\mathrm{H^{+}/D^{+}}$ separation factor of 10, which is much more efficient than conventional separation approaches.[80] Since then, 2D graphene and related 2D layered materials have been increasingly explored as promising candidates for application to proton diffusion and subatomic sieves for separating hydrogen isotopes at room temperature.[28,35,80,84,88,178–182]
Reportedly, hydrogen isotope storage and separation in graphene, h-BN, and MXenes are influenced by various key properties. In graphene, introducing atomic (e.g., 55–77 SW) defects to lattice rings may distribute the pore geometry and structure, which would effectively and selectively lower the hydrogen isotope permeation energy (E).[35] In addition, doping graphene with atoms such as N, B, C, or S or decorating graphene with high-hydrogen-affinity nanoparticles such as Pt, Pd, or Ni would reduce the hydrogen nanoparticle transport energy barrier.[79] In layered atomically thin h-BN, on the other hand, hydrogen isotope storage and separation are determined by the synergy among the h-BN specific area, micropores, B─N bond polarity, and high-density structural defects and vacancies.[183–187] In MXenes, hydrogen isotopes are reversibly adsorbed through Kubas-type interactions under ambient conditions, physisorbed and chemisorbed, and trapped in voids and encapsulated between the sub-nanoscale interlayer space through nanopumpeffect-assisted weak chemisorption.[183,188,189] More importantly, regular MXene nanometer channels act as an excellent sieve for separating gas molecules by manipulating the layer distance between adjacent sheets.[82,89] Experiments and simulations have shown that graphene, h-BN, and MXenes are excellent candidates for application to next-generation hydrogen isotope separation technologies. In this section, we highlight the current understanding of hydrogen isotope $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2},\ \operatorname{D}_{2}\right)$ , and particularly $\mathbf{T}_{2}$ ) storage and transport using 2-D layered materials such as graphene, h-BN, and MXenes and the storage, transport, and separation mechanisms and future challenges of these materials.
## 5.1. Graphene
Graphene is a one-atom-thick 2D $s p^{2}$ -bonded carbon sheet arranged in a hexagonal honeycomb lattice that exhibits ultrafast ion transport and has attracted tremendous interest for developing high-flux, ultrathin, and energy-efficient membranes for separating hydrogen isotopes.[79,190–194] Membrane separation performance is evaluated based on permeance and separation selectivity. First-principles DFT calculations and experiments realizations revealed that atomically thin graphene membrane subnanometer/micropores act as highly selective molecular sieves for transporting and selectively separating gases such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\Nu_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , Ar, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with high mobility, which depends on the pore size and functionalization.[190,195,196] Hankel et al. used ab initio calculations and kinetic modeling to develop an effective strategy to prepare asymmetrically decorated, doped porous graphene exhibiting QKS.[197] The authors reported that dressing one side of a graphene membrane with a metal such as lithium (Li) or titanium (Ti) provides a critical asymmetric low-energy sieving pathway favoring $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ -over- $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}$ separation selectivity. In a typical experiment using $1.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick graphene oxide (GO) membranes, Li et al. observed separation selectivities as high as 3400 and 900 for $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixtures, respectively.[191] Selectively introducing structural defects to the GO membrane increased the separation selectivity by one to two orders of magnitude compared to the selectivities of state-of-the-art microporous membranes. These findings suggested that the major transport pathway for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecular-sieving over other molecules was selective structural (e.g., intrinsic) defects in GO flakes, instead of the interlayer spacing between the GO flakes.[191] Furthermore, defect-free graphene or h-BN monolayers presumably exhibit subatomic selectivity and are permeable to thermal protons/electrons but impermeable to larger atomic species. In 2016, Lozada-Hidalgo et al. investigated the difference between proton $\left(\mathrm{H^{+}}\right)$ and deuteron $(\mathrm{D^{+}})$ permeation in graphene/h-BN monolayers exhibiting a high separation factor of 10 because the difference in the incident $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and $\mathsf{D}^{+}$ ZPEs corresponds to an equivalent difference in the 2-D graphene/h-BN activation barriers.[80] From the initial findings, the authors utilized graphene membranes as effective sieves for hydrogen isotope separation to design an electrochemical pump and explored the feasibility of applying the pump to industrially decontaminate tritium through a robust sieving mechanism,[28] which energy consumption is projected to be orders of magnitude lower than those of existing technologies. More importantly, the high selectivity $(\alpha\approx30)$ for tritium–hydrogen separation through the sieving mechanism was presumed by LozadaHidalgo et al.[80] In a typical experimental setup (Figure 12a), a proton- and deuteron-conducting Nafion membrane was sandwiched between Pd-decorated graphene and Pt-coated carbon cloth.[28] The graphene acts as both a cathode and a mixed proton–electron conductor, and the carbon cloth acts as an anode. By applying a bias of $\leq1.5\mathrm{V}$ across the device, the hydrogen isotopes (protons and deuterons) were electrochemically pumped through the Nafion and across graphene and recombined on the permeate side to form $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ , and protium deuteride (HD), which were detected using mass spectrometry.[28] The identified mechanism behind proton–deuteron sieving through graphenebased electrochemical pumping suggested a separation factor $(\alpha)$ of 30 for tritium–hydrogen separation. In addition, the authors presumed that utilizing $30~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ of graphene could process ${\approx}40$ tons of heavy water per year and that the expected efficiency would be higher for tritium separation. The energy $(E)$ consumed by electrochemical pumping for separating hydrogen isotopes was estimated at approximately $20\textup{G}$ J per $\mathrm{kg}$ of $20\%$ tritiumenriched heavy water with a separation factor of ${\approx}8$ , which outperformed all other isotope separation technologies offering low separation factors of only ${\approx}{<}1.8$ for separating hydrogen isotope mixtures. In addition, because electrochemical pumping does not face any fundamental obstacles or environmental issues pertaining to industrial scalability, electrochemical pumping has been proposed as a simple, effective, and highly efficient green technology for application to tritium separation in the nuclear power industry.[28] In addition, graphene is unique because its lattice can be reconstructed by forming nonhexagonal rings. For example, SW defects, which do not involve any removed or added atoms, play a crucial role in transporting protons and corresponding isotopes.[198] In contrast to previous reports[79,80,199] wherein defect-free 2-D graphene materials exhibited a separation factor $(\alpha)$ of ${\approx}10$ at room temperature, a recent study by An et al.[35] used the Wentzel-Kramers-Brillouin (WKB) method to approximate quantum tunneling and estimate the flow of protons and corresponding isotopes through pristine graphene exhibiting 55–77 SW topological defects and a locally hydrogenated ring. The authors hypothesized that proton transfer through atomically thin graphene membranes was governed by tunneling through seven-membered rings in the 55–77 SW defects and in the grain boundaries. They reported that ideally, graphene is quasi-impermeable to protons and that a topological SW defect exhibits a calculated penetration barrier below $1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and an $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ over- $\cdot\mathsf{D}^{+}$ selectivity of 7 at room temperature (Figure 12b), which explains all the available experimental results on graphene membranes. The calculated tritium selectivities suggest that because graphene exhibits a $\mathrm{H^{+}/T^{+}}$ selectivity of 12 under ambient conditions, it may be useful for enriching tritium concentrations in contaminated water.[35] More importantly, because GO membranes exhibit lower energy consumptions and higher separation efficiencies than other hydrogen isotope separation technologies, GO membranes have been explored as potential candidates for purifying low-concentration HTO $(10^{-3}{-}10\upmu\mathrm{Ci}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Owing to its advantages, graphene-based hydrogen isotope (especially tritium) separation seems very important compared to other separation technologies in the highly conservative nuclear power industry.
In the nuclear energy sector, the separation of light water/tritiated water $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{HTO})$ is an important process for treating tritium-containing wastewater. Recently, Li et al. successfully achieved the enhanced separation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with increased permeation flux by tuning of interlayer spacing and structure of graphene oxide (GO) through intercalation and crosslinking strategies.[31] The authors used cations $(\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ , or $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ ), or cationic surfactant-cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) to vary the interlayer spacing of GO via intercalation. The GO membrane intercalated with $\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}$ ions (GO-Ca) gave the highest permeation flux of $0.87\ \mathrm{kg\m^{-2}\ h^{-1}}$ with a separation factor of $4.6\%$ . By contrast, the GO-framework (GOF) membrane prepared by cross-linking of GO nanosheets with $\mathbb{A}^{1^{3+}}$ cations (GOFAl) achieved a separation factor of $4.9\%$ and a permeation flux of $0.9\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . The performances of both GOs obtained through intercalation and cross-linking were higher than those of pristine GO membranes. This strategy of tuning the interlayer space of GO through intercalation and cross-linking can guide the future development of low-cost and efficient $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{HTO}$ separation processes needed in the nuclear energy field.[31]
## 5.2. Hexagonal Boron Nitride (h-BN)
In contrast to carbonaceous or zeolitic materials that cryoadsorb $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , boron nitride (BN) physisorbed and/or chemisorbs considerable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at room temperature (mainly between $-196~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ), as demonstrated by experimental observations and theoretical calculations, which makes BN so peculiar and worth investigating more deeply for application to the imminent “hydrogen economy” (Table S5, Supporting Information).[186,200–203] Boron nitrite usually exists as graphite-like hexagonal BN (h-BN), rhombohedral BN (r-BN), wurtzite BN (w-BN), and diamond-like cubic BN (c-BN) phases.
h-BN is a remarkable 2D mesh consisting of vdW-coupled layers of $s p^{2}$ -hybridized boron and nitrogen atoms alternating in a honeycomb lattice exhibiting a lattice constant of $2.50\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Because they are graphene structural analogs, h-BN nanosheets (hBN NSs) are also known as “white graphene.”[79,187,204] To date, substantial progress has been made in developing h-BN nanomaterials exhibiting various structural morphologies such as 0- D nanoparticles; 1-D nanotubes,[205,206] nanomeshes, nanofibers, and nanoribbons; 2-D nanosheets and nanomeshes; and 3-D hollow spheres, nanoflowers, and nanocages. Additionally, the hydrogen uptake capacities of these materials have been widely studied in real-time experiments and computational analyses, and most findings and predictions have indicated hydrogen uptakes between 2 and $8\mathrm{\mt\%}$ at RT.[186,187,203] The experimental findings and calculated theoretical predictions reveal that both the large specific surface area and higher micropore $(<2\ \mathrm{nm})$ content constituting $595\%$ of the h-BN void space facilitate hydrogen physisorption (Figure 13a).[207,208] Moreover, the extra dipole moment attributed to the ionic $_{\mathrm{B-N}}$ bond promotes hydrogen chemisorption because hydrogen transfers electrons to the electron-deficient B atoms.[209,210] Further, high-density defect sites such as h-BN SW defects and vacancies lead to deviation from $s p^{2}$ -hybridized bonding, which in turn leads to higher hydrogen chemisorption binding energies.[187,211] In particular, defects enhance $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ dissociation and H atom adsorption. In addition, substitutional doping, wherein B and/or N are/is substituted by C or O (or both), positively impacts the hydrogen–hBN binding energy.[186,211] More importantly, Ti,[212] V, Pt,[213] and $\mathrm{Pd}^{[214]}$ transition-metal doping resulted in high reversible hydrogen storage at room temperature because the transition-metal 3d orbitals hybridized with the H 1s orbital. By synergistically implementing quantum delocalization and strong confinement within small nanotubes to differentiate hydrogen isotopes, Kowalczyk et al. achieved QS in the quasi-1D pores of single-walled BN, which efficiently differentiated hydrogen isotopes with high selectivity at $33\mathrm{~K~}$ because hydrogen isotopes had quantumly delocalized in the single-walled BN nanotube interior space.[215] Because lighter hydrogen isotopes exhibit higher quantum entropies (e.g., hydrogen molecules exhibit higher zero-point motion than deuterium or tritium molecules), lighter hydrogen isotopes are more delocalized than heavier ones.[215]
One-atom-thick h-BN monolayers, the building block of all h-BN nanomaterials, should be a promising candidate for application to future hydrogen isotope (especially tritium) separation technologies[79] because h-BN monolayers exhibit 1) the best porosity among all the other 2D materials including graphene and ${\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ because electrons are highly concentrated around N atoms, which renders $_{\mathrm{B-N}}$ bonds highly polarized;[79] 2) the highest proton conductivity and high thermal proton permeability under ambient conditions. Accelerated hydrogen atoms but not hydrogen molecules exhibit sufficient kinetic energy to permeate h-BN planes without damaging the lattices. In addition, monolayer h-BN reportedly exhibits the highest proton conductivity, followed by bilayer h-BN and monolayer graphene;[79,187,204,216] 3) chemical stability, high flexibility, air stability up to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , high mechanical strength, and resistance against extreme conditions such as those in nuclear reactors;[203,204] 4) small bending moduli and may conformationally change owing to molecular physisorption.[217]
Hu et al. investigated the possibility of proton transport through monocrystalline membranes fabricated using monoand few-layer graphenes, h-BN, and molybdenum disulfide $(\mathrm{MoS}_{2})$ (Figure 13b).[79] They reported that the graphene and hBN monolayers were highly permeable to thermal protons under ambient conditions with monolayer h-BN exhibiting the highest room-temperature proton conductivity, whereas thicker crystals such as monolayer $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ , bilayer graphene, or multilayer hBN did not exhibit any proton transport (Figure 13c).[79] In addition, the authors reported that decorating h-BN with catalytic nanoparticles enhanced the proton transport. Because atoms behave like waves rather than classical particles in confined spaces commensurate with their de Broglie wavelengths, Hu et al. recently separated hydrogen isotopes by sieving them through hBN $(\approx3.34\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ $(\approx6.15\mathrm{~\AA})$ interstitial spaces.[84] The authors fabricated a device to investigate the sieving of thermal protons and deuterons (e.g., hydrogen isotope nuclei) through vdW gaps. In the experimental setup (Figure 13d), h-BN was placed on a freestanding silicon nitride $(\mathrm{SiN}_{x})$ membrane and coated with a thin Pd film (to prepare a good proton transport interface) and was then covered on both sides with a proton-conducting Nafion membrane.[84] High proton and negligible Nafion electron conductivities were ensured by placing the device in a chamber with $10\%\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in an Ar atmosphere at $100\%$ humidity. Subsequently, h-BN proton permeation was analyzed by applying a potential between the electrodes (Figure $_{13\mathrm{e-g}}$ .[84] Because deuterium exhibits a shorter de Broglie wavelength $(\lambda_{\mathrm{B}}^{\mathrm{D}}\approx1.02\mathring\mathrm{A})$ than protons $(\lambda_{\mathrm{B}}^{\mathrm{H}}\approx1.45\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , deuterons face a lower entry energy barrier than protons, which results in a separation factor of 1.4 at room temperature. The energy consumption was greatly reduced because the working environment was simply ambient conditions and did not require cryogenic temperatures. This experiment reveals that layered materials exhibiting proper interstitial spaces could, therefore, serve as quantum sieves for separating hydrogen isotopes, especially tritium (T), which exhibits a much shorter de Broglie wavelength than D or H.[84]
The aligned porously stacked h-BN multilayer makes h-BN transparent to atomic hydrogen but impermeable to hydrogen molecules. He et al. used plasma treatment to extract hydrogen gases from hydrogen-containing mixtures and isolated atomic hydrogen between h-BN layers.[204] Atomic hydrogen produced by plasma-treatment-induced $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ dissociation permeates the hBN plane and is then captured inside the h-BN interlayers. The authors designed an experimental setup, as shown in Figure $^{13\mathrm{h}}$ , in which the h-BN surface was bombarded with plasma in different gaseous (Ar, $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , or $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ) environments. Subjecting gaseous molecules to a strong electromagnetic field produces masses of charged ions exhibiting particle radii much smaller than those of the original atoms. Because the hydrogen atoms exhibited a diameter of ${\approx}240\ \mathrm{pm}$ , which is smaller than the h-BN lattice constant $\mathrm{\langle\approx250.4~pm\rangle}$ ), they easily penetrated the h-BN. However, the hydrogen, argon, and oxygen molecules exhibited diameters of ${\approx}289$ , ${\approx}376$ , and ${\approx}304~\mathrm{pm}$ , respectively, which are larger than the h-BN lattice constant. Therefore, these molecules could not permeate h-BN. In addition, the authors reported that the atomic hydrogen penetration depth reached approximately $23\mathrm{h}$ - BN layers. Because hydrogen isotopes (H, D, and T) exhibit larger mass and size differences, layered h-BN can be effectively utilized to separate/store protium, deuterium, and tritium.[204] Applying these membranes to a current-controlled hydrogen source is also appealing for its simplicity: once large-area graphene and h-BN films become commercially available, they can be used to extract hydrogen from gas mixtures or air.[79]
## 5.3. MXenes
Developing tritium storage materials exhibiting high reversible hydrogen storage capacity and high hydrogen isotope separation efficiency is a major challenge and a long-term goal for materials scientists and chemists. MXenes have recently emerged as an intriguing family of 2-D graphene-like layer-structured transitionmetal carbides, nitrides, and carbonitrides. They exhibit the chemical formula $\mathbf{M}_{n+1}\mathbf{X}_{n}\mathbf{T}_{x}$ ( $n=1$ , 2, or 3), where M represents an early $d$ -block transition metal (e.g., Ti, Sc, V, Cr, Mo, Zr, Hf, Nb, Ta, or W), X represents carbon (C) and/or nitrogen (N), and $\mathrm{T}_{x}$ denotes surface groups such as $-0$ , ─OH, or ─F.[86,218–221] Because MXenes exhibit unique structural characteristics such as large specific surface areas, layered structures with adjustable interlamellar channels, and numerous functional groups, MXenes occupy a niche in reversible hydrogen storage and separation under ambient conditions. More importantly, MXenes resist radiationinduced damage, which is a prerequisite for application to hydrogen storage and separation materials in fusion reactors and nuclear industries.[222–225] Gogotsi et al. discovered the titanium (III) carbide $(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x})$ MXene in 2011.[218,220] Since then, more than 70 MAX phases and 30 MXene compositions have been reported including $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ ,[226] $\mathrm{Ti}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{3}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ ,[227] $\mathsf{N b}_{4}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ ,[228] $\mathrm{Lu}_{2}\mathrm{C},$ [229] $\mathrm{Zr}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ ,[230] and $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{x}$ ,[231] and many more possibilities have been explored through theoretical computations.[220,224,232,233]
As hydrogen storage materials, transition-element-containing MXenes are important because their hydrogen storage is reversible under ambient conditions because Kubas-type interactions reversibly add $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to transition metal complexes at room temperature through weak chemisorption. Kubas-type interactions are associated with the $\operatorname{H}_{2}\sigma$ orbital donating electrons to empty transition metal d orbitals. The electrons are simultaneously back-donated from the filled metal d orbitals to the $\operatorname{H}_{2}\sigma$ antibonding orbitals while exhibiting binding energies in the range $0.2\mathrm{-}0.8\mathrm{eV}$ molecule−1 $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (which is required for reversible hydrogen storage in the range $300{-}400\mathrm{K}$ and at ambient pressure). This range falls between those required for weak physisorption and strong chemisorption when the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule adsorption energy is as low as the energy required for reversible hydrogen storage at RT and ambient pressure.[86,88,188] Thus, MXenes exhibiting Kubas-type hydrogen storage should be a promising option for reversibly storing tritium under ambient conditions, and more research is required in this area. Toward that goal, the previously reported experimental and dynamic MS studies on hydrogen storage in 2-D MXenes may be useful starting points. Further research should be devoted to this area to drive important scientific developments.[232,234]
Using density functional calculations, Yadav et al. studied the possibility of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule adsorption at various sites on 2-D $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ MXene surfaces. They reported a hydrogen storage capacity of $7.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in which $3.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of the H was attributed to Kubastype interactions, $1.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ was bonded through chemisorption, and $3.2~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ was bonded through weak electrostatic interactions (Figure 14a).[235] Similar simulation results have been obtained for 2-D $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ ,[188] $\mathsf{S c}_{2}\mathsf C,$ [236] and $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{N}^{[237]}$ MXenes (Table S6, Supporting Information), which demonstrated that Sc-, Ti-, and V-based MXene materials exhibit promising reversible and high-gravimetric hydrogen storage capacities under ambient conditions and are far higher than the gravimetric capacity of metal-based complex hydrides $(\approx5.5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , as set by the US DOE (2017).[238] Using experimental measurements and elastic neutron-scattering techniques, Osti and Gogotsi et al. reported that $0.3\mathrm{wt}\%$ hydrogen (which is approximately $25\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules per $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene formula unit) was trapped in the voids (e.g., small cavities) of pristine $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ under ambient conditions.[189] Furthermore, from detailed experimental studies on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ and $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ MXenes, Liu et al. reported highly reversible room-temperature hydrogen storage in multilayered ${\mathrm{Ti}}_{2}{\mathrm{CT}}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ stacks exhibiting a narrow interlayer distance of ${\approx}7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure 14b) and abundant fluorine $(-\mathrm{F})$ groups.[88] The $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ stacks were synthesized using an incomplete hydrofluoric acid (HF) etching strategy and exhibited an unprecedented hydrogen uptake of $8.8\mathrm{wt\%}$ at room temperature and 60 bar $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Figure 14c,d). This is more than double the capacity of previously reported room-temperature hydrogen storage materials at the same pressure and may be attributed to the nanopumpeffect-assisted weak hydrogen chemisorption caused by the material subnanoscale interlayer space. Even under completely ambient conditions (e.g., $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 1 bar air), $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{CT}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ still retained $\approx4\uppi\uprho\%$ hydrogen. The material exhibited stable and reversible hydrogen storage, and the hydrogen release was controlled by pressure and temperatures below $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Such hydrogen storage approaches provide a promising strategy for designing a wide range of nanomaterials, especially those exhibiting many subnanopores and chemically modifiable inner surfaces. More importantly, the unique layer-structured metallic MXenes $\lbrack\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ , $(\mathrm{Ti}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{0.5})_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Nb}_{4}\mathrm{C}_{3}$ , and NbTiC] formed in situ play synergistic catalytic and framework-confining roles to considerably promote the hydrogen storage capacities of metal hydrides such as $\mathrm{LiBH}_{4}$ ,[239–241] $\mathrm{MgH}_{2}$ ,[242–246] $\mathrm{Mg(BH}_{4})_{2}$ ,[247,248] and $\mathrm{LiAlH_{4}}$ [249] by lowering their desorption temperatures and improving dehydrogenation (Table S7, Supporting Information).[245,246,250] These reports show that MXenes exhibit remarkable hydrogen adsorption capabilities. Because tritium is chemically analogous to hydrogen, more research should be devoted to MXene-based tritium storage materials, which will drive important scientific developments.
The 2-D membranes exhibiting highly ordered nanochannels can enable rapid and precise molecular sieving for gas separation. In contrast to intrinsic- or artificial-pore-containing zeolite-, silica-, MOF-, and graphene-based molecular sieving membranes as molecular sieving channels, which exhibit low selectivity and poor sieving for smaller molecules at high temperatures, layered 2-D-structured MXenes exhibit tunable physiochemical properties, facile synthesis methods, and easy large-scale fabrication. This enables separation systems to be tailored to remove numerous gaseous molecules and is especially suitable for separating hydrogen isotopes under ambient conditions. The excellent separation and exceptional high-temperature stability of 2-D
## 6. Conclusions and Future Perspectives
## 6.1. Metal Hydrides
Tritium $(\mathrm{T}_{2})$ storage materials are crucial nuclear fusion reactor components. However, no current $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ storage material has met the standards required for industrial application. High-entropy alloys (HEAs), formed by combining five or more metallic elements, have been proposed based on a completely new concept, and have emerged as excellent $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ storage alloys with high capacity, excellent He retention ability, and exceptional stability thanks to their distorted lattice arrangements, which may bring $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ storage materials into a new era. It is believed that these new $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ storage materials can be developed based on the new material design concept and the understanding of the helium growth mechanism in the materials. Furthermore, research on $\mathrm{HEAT}_{2}$ storage capacities for application to $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ storage materials is still in its infancy and deserves great attention. More investigations are warranted to understand the maximum capacity of hydrogen storage, absorption and desorption kinetics, and stability, as well as ease of utilization of stored hydrogen, in order to develop multicomponent HEAs as the most efficient and effective hydrogen energy storage materials. As a representative material, ZrCo-based alloys have been used in nuclear reactor storage beds for storing $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ , thanks to their low pyrophoricity and higher storage capacity. Nevertheless, the $\mathrm{ZrCo}$ alloy disproportionation has been minimized by substituting other elements and modifying the alloy surface. Further research is required to optimize the methods for preparing alloy compositions. More importantly, research should focus on enhancing $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ uptake by stabilizing metal tritides at approximately ambient temperature and pressure. In addition, the
He atoms generated during the decay of radioactive $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ atoms and their evolution during aging seriously affect the macroscopic properties of $\mathrm{~T}_{2}$ storage materials. Systematic studies focusing on the evolution mechanism of the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ and He have rarely been reported. On the other hand, a wide range of experimental and modeling studies on the effects of the material genome, elemental composition, and lattice distortion on $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ storage and its thermodynamics may lead to the development of next-generation $\mathrm{T}_{2}$ storage materials.
## 6.2. Porous Materials (MOFs and Zeolites)
Elucidating nanospace QS and tailoring porous materials accordingly will provide opportunities to develop highly efficient and advanced hydrogen isotope separation systems.
## 6.2.1. MOFs
MOFs exhibiting promise for practical industrial application to tritium storage and separation present a formidable yet exceedingly complex and engaging challenge. MOFs are broadly applied to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage and hydrogen isotope separation via both CAQS and KQS, owing to their abundant adsorption sites, high surface area, and diverse pore structures. $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 11.1 at $100~\mathrm{K}$ has been achieved on $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -MFU-4l, and KQS and CAQS can be combined on imidazole molecules (IM) modified MOF74 (MOF-74IM) attaining a $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 26 at 77 K. Currently, flexible MOFs, capable of molecular recognition of physicochemically similar mixtures are in a new era for smart separation materials, which opens new avenues for separating hydrogen isotopes. Future challenges in the field of hydrogen isotope separation include the development of a realistic separation platform for practical applications based on previous findings. Achieving hydrogen isotope separation with high selectivity via CAQS through the introduction of strong unsaturated metal sites (UMSs) or metal dopants (Co, Ni, Mg, Mn, Fe, Zn) is well established. However, there have been no reports on alkali earth metaldoped MOFs for hydrogen isotope separation, which is expected to achieve an excellent hydrogen isotope through CAQS. High $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ capacity along with high selectivity is an important parameter for realizing isotope separation in practical applications, which has been achieved using Hofmann-type MOFs with the highest OMS density. In the future, Hofmann-type MOFs having the highest OMS density are worth investigating to observe the CAQS effect. Mesoporous MOFs have the advantages of including more accessible OMSs for the preferential combination of heavier $\mathsf{D}_{2}$ based on CAQS and large pores for increasing the diffusion rate of the hydrogen isotope. More information is needed to explore hydrogen isotope separation using mesoporous MOFs compared to microporous MOFs. Flexible MOFs can selectively respond to external stimuli, adapting their pore size adsorption-induced gating or breathing. Based on the delicate energy balance of a flexible MOF, selective adsorption of protium-free dihydrogen isotopologues with high selectivity has been achieved through linker functionalization. These kinds of flexible MOFs offer a path to highly purified heavy hydrogen and are expected to be competitive, compared not only to cryogenic distillation but also to alternative 2D material-based isotope selective membranes. Microscopic observation of isotopic molecular transportation in flexible MOFs during the separation process under dynamic transition plays a vital role in elucidating the mechanism and enabling the design novel KQS MOF adsorbents, which has been achieved in only few MOFs. Thus, microscopic observation of diffusivity to gas separation should be investigated either experimentally (e.g., pulsed-field-gradient (PFG) NMR, quasi-electron neutron scattering (QENS), and DRIFT spectroscopy) or through molecular dynamic simulations. The structural stability of MOFs plays an essential role in hydrogen isotope separation applications. Only a few MOFs (UiO, MIL, ZIFs) are structurally highly stable. Screening preeminent candidates for tritium storage and separation capability and high stability from numerous MOFs endowed with infinite combinations of metal ions and organic linkers is extremely challenging. Therefore, simulation and modeling studies should be undertaken to rigorously solve problems involving all the complexities while attempting to generate MOFs possessing enhanced tritium storage and separation. More importantly, the inability to form strong metal–ligand bonds in open MOFs to support permanent porosity in solvents and the subsequent MOF pore structural collapse and adventitious moisture hinders the application of MOFs to gas storage and separation. Thus, developing MOF design strategies is critical to prepare MOFs that exhibit structural rigidity by adding linkers with different characteristics to stabilize framework structures with larger apparent surface areas and pore volumes and excellent tritium storage and separation capabilities prior to commercial deployment.
## 6.2.2. Zeolites
Notably, the discovery of zeolite frameworks exhibiting unprecedented structural features and hierarchical, chiral, and extra-large pore structures, appropriate exchangeable cations; and extremely complex framework topologies approximating the kinetic diameter of hydrogen may yield options for tritium storage and separation using enriched zeolite structures. Metal ion-exchanged zeolites such as $\mathrm{Cu(I)}{\cdot}\mathrm{ZSM}{\cdot}5$ and Ag(I)-ZSM have shown excellent selectivities of 24.9 (at $100~\mathrm{K}$ for 1:1 $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixture) and 3.4 (at 77 K for 1:1 $\mathrm{T}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}^{\cdot}$ , respectively, and are predicted to be used to enrich and separate tritium from its isotope mixtures in a technologically favorable temperature range. Currently, ion-exchanged zeolites with higher mass density of strong adsorption sites are considered as potent candidates for hydrogen isotope separation. The control of the CAQS effect of active sites in the pore interiors of easily scalable zeolites has potential for the separation of hydrogen isotopes. However, little research on hydrogen isotope separation based on the CAQS effect in zeolites has been reported. Thus, the development of ion-exchanged zeolites, especially dihydrogen bond on transition metal ion $(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{I})$ , Ni(II), Fe(II), and $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I}).$ )-exchanged zeolites, offers a potential practical means of hydrogen isotope separation and enrichment on a technological scale. Zeolites with a higher mass density of strong adsorption sites exhibit a higher adsorption capacity and higher selectivity (above liquid nitrogen temperature) for heavy hydrogen isotopes than those with a low density of exchanged ions. Nevertheless, there are only a few studies on zeolites with a higher density of adsorption sites, and more research is needed in this area.
Computational chemistry techniques should be implemented to rationally design zeolite structures with desired pore structures and elucidate the molecular formation mechanisms. Rapidly emerging MS analyses should be implemented to elucidate tritium molecular adsorption and separation mechanisms for which insufficient experimental information is available and to evaluate the potential of applying zeolites to tritium adsorption and separation materials.
## 6.3. Layered Materials (Graphene, h-BN, and MXenes)
2D-material membranes with well-defined transport channels and ultrathin thicknesses have demonstrated extraordinary water and gas separation performances.
## 6.3.1. Graphene
Atomically thin graphene, through the implementation of electrochemical pumping technology, has demonstrated a separation factor of 30 for $\mathrm{T}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . In addition, it has been presumed that the use of $30~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ graphene is capable of processing more than 40 tons of heavy water per annum for tritium separation while expending less energy. More importantly, the sieving of hydrogen isotopes resulting from the presence of Stone-Wales (SW) defects in graphene sheets is responsible for an $\mathrm{H^{+}/T^{+}}$ selectivity of 12 at ambient conditions, which is an important finding. Practically applying graphene to tritium separation remains a technical challenge, and the theoretically predicted and experimentally measured adsorption parameters are considerably different. The current challenges that restrict the wide implementation of graphene as an efficient tritium separation membrane include the following: i) the artificial creation of uniform sub-nanopores through etching; ii) the limited techniques for exfoliating bulk graphene into intact nanoporous monolayers with high aspect ratios, iii) the precise manipulation of intergallery dimensions, and iv) scaling atomically thin graphene to fabricate tritium separation devices. Thus, considerable effort should be exerted to develop robust graphene membranes with excellent tritium separation performances under realistic operating conditions.
## 6.3.2. Hexagonal Boron Nitride (h-BN)
According to experimental and theoretical results of hydrogenbased adsorption and permeability studies, BN-based materials are forecasted as candidates for application to tritium storage and separation. h-BN nanosheets exhibit small bending moduli and may conformationally change to enhance the surface adsorption of molecules. Currently, experimental methods cannot be used to straightforwardly detect atomic morphological changes when adsorbates have covered BN nanosheets. Morphological changes should be validated through advanced techniques and theoretical predictions because the surface adsorption behaviors must be elucidated to effectively develop efficient tritium storage and separation applications. Nevertheless, heteroatom doping, catalytic metal nanoparticle decoration, functional group grafting, and vacancy engineering synergies should generate more adsorption sites exhibiting elevated binding energies for proton transport and hydrogen adsorption. The strong chemical interaction between hydrogen and h-BN negatively impacts the release of hydrogen, which seems to be the most critical material drawback. More importantly, substantial experimental efforts are required to design h-BN layers exhibiting appropriate micro/mesopores to efficiently store and separate tritium. However, h-BN exhibits excellent tritium separation behavior, and large-area h-BN films are not commercially available, which limits their practical industrial application on an industrial scale. More importantly, the potential h-BN toxicity and the tradeoff between h-BN selectivity and permeability remain unclear.
## 6.3.3. MXenes
MXenes provide a new route for the development of molecular sieving membranes due to the presence of sufficient and uniform nanochannels that break the permeability–selectivity tradeoff and are desirable for energy-efficient hydrogen isotope separation. MXene membrane permeability and selectivity outperform those of top-of-the-line membrane materials, provided the intergallery dimensions are precisely manipulated. However, the interlayer nanochannels, which aid mass transport in MXenes, are usually formed in a disordered fashion between randomly stacked adjacent nanosheets, which makes highly efficient separation difficult. Therefore, MXene lamellar membranes exhibiting highly ordered nanochannel structures must be prepared to efficiently and precisely sieve hydrogen isotopes, which remains a challenge. Furthermore, theoretical models should be developed to accurately determine the confined transport behavior of molecules through 2-D MXene membranes with an in-depth characterization of transport passages.
This concise review of state-of-the-art tritium storage and separation materials leads to many conclusions. We are convinced that the potential of metal hydrides, zeolites, MOFs, graphene, hBN, and MXene materials toward tritium storage and separation is enormous, although each class offers its own perspective for improving performance, and many practical challenges remain to be overcome. We hope that this review will provide diverse insights into the tritium storage and separation research status and a strong impetus to further develop these systems for future research. Moreover, we hope that it would help scientists be optimistic about the implications of these discoveries and continue to investigate these metal hydride, porous, and layered materials in combination with other materials for application to tritium storage and separation.
