Muruganantham Rethinasabapathy is a principal researcher in the Department of Biological Engineering at INHA University, Korea. He received his Ph.D. degree in physics from Anna University, India in 2015. He has expertise in energy and environmental nanomaterials. His research interests focus on the development of functional nanomaterials for energy conversion and storage technologies (fuel cells, supercapacitors, etc.), and environmental remediation (removal of radionuclides such as tritium, cesium, and strontium, etc. from the environment).
Seyed Majid Ghoreishian is currently a senior researcher at the Department of Chemical Engineering, University of South Carolina, USA. He received his Ph.D. degree from the INHA University, Korea under the supervision of Prof. Yun Suk Huh in 2021. He is committed to the research of environmental catalysis and his main research includes atomic-level photocatalysis and photoelectrocatalysis.
Seung-Kyu Hwang received his B.Sc and M.S degree in engineering chemistry from Kangwon National University, Korea. He received his Ph.D. degree from INHA University in 2022, with a dissertation focused on radioactive removal and supercapacitor. Currently, he is an assistant professor at INHA University. His research interests focus on the development of adsorption materials for the removal of radioactive substances and the synthesis and characterization of novel materials for energy storage and conversion applications.
Young-Kyu Han received his Ph.D. degree in the Department of Chemistry, KAIST, Daejeon, Republic of Korea in 1999. Now he is a full professor in the Department of Energy and Materials Engineering at Dongguk University, Seoul, Republic of Korea. His current research interests are the atomic/molecular quantum simulation of organic and inorganic hybrid materials. He is also working on the design of new promising materials based on atomic-level simulation.
Changhyun Roh is a principal researcher in Decommissioning Technology Division, Korea Atomic Energy Research Institute, Korea. And he is also working in nuclear science and technology from the University of Science and Technology. He received his B.S. and M.S. degrees in chemical engineering from CNU and his Ph.D. degree (2007) from SNU in chemical engineering. After working as a post-doc researcher in material engineering from Auburn University USA, he joined the faculty at KAERI (2008). His research interests include separation of radioisotopes, nanomaterials, and nuclear science and technology.
Yun Suk Huh received his Ph.D. degree in the Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering, KAIST, Daejeon, Republic of Korea, in 2007. Now he is a full professor in the Department of Biological Science and Bioengineering at Inha University, Incheon, Republic of Korea. His research interest includes the development of energy storage and conversion technologies, microfluidics, bio-hybrid materials for drug delivery and bioimaging, triboelectric nanogenerators, and nuclear technology.
